esult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/xling_calib_direction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T201854/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Reframe as field-level empirical survey: \"Does probe-based calibration\n  work beyond English? A systematic investigation across 2 architectures and 20 languages.\"\n  Three surviving novel contributions: (1) mean-shift rank-invariance theorem with\n  proof — explains why mean-shift fails (ΔAUROC ≡ 0 under StandardScaler + logistic\n  regression), distinguishing from Xiao et al.; (2) resource-tier taxonomy of cross-lingual\n  probe transferability — first quantification across 20 languages showing high-resource\n  transfers, mid is marginal, low fails; (3) practical recommendation — few-shot (25–100\n  labels) is the only viable path, closing the zero-label dream for low-resource languages.\n  CaCLR is relegated to one paragraph as attempted-and-failed (consistency loss inert,\n  SB-ECE alone insufficient). Decomposition is reported as inconclusive (difficulty\n  removal neither helps nor hurts transfer systematically). Pareto frontier is real\n  on Qwen3 but flat/non-monotonic on Llama 4 Scout — architecture-dependent, not universal.\n  CKA provides suggestive but not convincing mechanistic explanation (r < 0.45). Orthogonality\n  persists broadly but with outliers (cosine up to 0.17 for low-resource), weakening\n  the universality claim. Control tasks reveal capacity artifacts in low-resource\n  settings (selectivity < 8pp). Paper becomes a \"sobering empirical reality check\"\n  for the probe-based calibration line. Target: borderline at EACL, requires very\n  clean writing and strong related-work positioning.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Full Pareto Frontier — Qwen3-235B-A22B (94 layers, MoE, INT8)\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: 'Extract activations at 24 probed layers (every 4th of 94), 20 languages\n    on GlobalMMLU. Train English logistic regression + 2-layer MLP correctness probes.\n    Evaluate 5 transfer conditions: zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection, few-shot\n    (25/100), CaCLR. Report per-language Spearman ρ (depth vs transfer AUROC) alongside\n    global ρ. Bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) on all AUROC/ECE; paired permutation\n    tests for method comparisons; Cohen''s d for effect sizes.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.776\n    en_best_layer_idx: 72\n    en_auroc_range:\n    - 0.612\n    - 0.776\n    en_ece_raw_best: 0.174\n    en_ece_tempcal_best: 0.069\n    temp_scaling_T: 5.91\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.618\n    zs_transfer_auroc_median_19lang: 0.618\n    zs_transfer_auroc_std_19lang: 0.046\n    zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 15.8\n    zs_langs_above_0p65: 7\n    zs_pass_langs:\n    - zh\n    - fr\n    - de\n    - es\n    - ja\n    - ko\n    - ru\n    zs_fail_langs:\n    - ar\n    - hi\n    - vi\n    - id\n    - th\n    - pl\n    - uk\n    - bn\n    - te\n    - ta\n    - sw\n    - yo\n    mean_shift_delta_auroc_all_langs: 0.0\n    mean_shift_delta_ece_unreliable: true\n    mean_shift_delta_ece_range_pp:\n    - -1.2\n    - 6.8\n    linproj_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.636\n    linproj_improves_over_zs_count: 11\n    linproj_delta_auroc_vs_zs_mean_pp: 1.8\n    fewshot25_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.644\n    fewshot25_improves_over_zs_count: 14\n    fewshot100_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.662\n    fewshot100_improves_over_zs_count: 16\n    pareto_global_spearman_rho: -0.38\n    pareto_per_lang_rho_median: -0.25\n    pareto_per_lang_rho_range:\n    - -0.55\n    - 0.31\n    pareto_rho_sign_consistency: 0.63\n    pareto_frontier_n_nondominated: 5\n    pareto_frontier_monotonic: false\n    bootstrap_ci_width_auroc_median_pp: 2.8\n    per_lang_zs_auroc:\n      zh: 0.668\n      fr: 0.672\n      de: 0.671\n      es: 0.661\n      ja: 0.658\n      ko: 0.656\n      ru: 0.652\n      ar: 0.625\n      hi: 0.618\n      vi: 0.607\n      id: 0.621\n      th: 0.594\n      pl: 0.628\n      uk: 0.614\n      bn: 0.553\n      te: 0.571\n      ta: 0.568\n      sw: 0.537\n      yo: 0.512\n    per_lang_best_zs_layer:\n      zh: 52\n      fr: 52\n      de: 52\n      es: 48\n      ja: 48\n      ko: 48\n      ru: 44\n      ar: 40\n      hi: 40\n      vi: 40\n      id: 40\n      th: 36\n      pl: 44\n      uk: 40\n      bn: 28\n      te: 28\n      ta: 28\n      sw: 28\n      yo: 24\n    per_lang_spearman_rho:\n      zh: -0.55\n      fr: -0.51\n      de: -0.48\n      es: -0.42\n      ja: -0.37\n      ko: -0.33\n      ru: -0.25\n      ar: -0.18\n      hi: -0.11\n      pl: -0.14\n      uk: -0.06\n      id: -0.02\n      vi: 0.04\n      th: 0.09\n      bn: 0.14\n      te: 0.18\n      ta: 0.22\n      sw: 0.27\n      yo: 0.31\n    transfer_by_resource_tier:\n      high_mean: 0.663\n      high_langs:\n      - zh\n      - fr\n      - de\n      - es\n      - ja\n      - ko\n      - ru\n      mid_mean: 0.615\n      mid_langs:\n      - ar\n      - hi\n      - vi\n      - id\n      - th\n      - pl\n      - uk\n      low_mean: 0.548\n      low_langs:\n      - bn\n      - te\n      - ta\n      - sw\n      - yo\n  result_rationale: 'Qwen3-235B improves only modestly over the MVP''s Qwen2.5-7B\n    (EN AUROC 0.776 vs 0.754) because gains from scale are sublinear for linear probing\n    — the larger model has more complex, distributed representations where correctness\n    is encoded across many dimensions rather than in a single cleanly accessible direction.\n    The Pareto frontier exists but is noisy: global Spearman ρ = -0.38 means depth\n    explains ~15% of transfer variance. Per-language rho varies dramatically — high-resource\n    languages that share subspace structure with English (zh, fr, de) show clear negative\n    correlation (deeper = worse transfer), while low-resource languages (sw, yo, ta)\n    show positive or near-zero rho because their best transfer happens at shallow\n    layers where representations are still language-agnostic, and the signal is too\n    weak at any depth for a clear depth gradient to emerge. MoE expert routing introduces\n    non-monotonic noise at mid-depth layers (36-56) where routing patterns shift.\n    Mean-shift ΔAUROC is exactly 0.000 across all languages and layers, confirming\n    the rank-invariance proof: under StandardScaler + logistic regression, mean subtraction\n    adds a constant to all logits, preserving ranks. ECE changes from mean-shift are\n    unreliable because they depend on threshold-sensitive binning of shifted probabilities.\n    Linear projection helps 11/19 languages by learning the affine transformation\n    between language subspaces, but fails for low-resource languages where insufficient\n    parallel data quality prevents reliable transformation estimation. Few-shot-100\n    is the best method but requires per-language labels, partially negating the zero-label\n    promise.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: Qwen3-235B English Probe Performance by Layer (Selected Layers)'\n    columns:\n    - layer\n    - depth_pct\n    - logreg_auroc\n    - mlp_auroc\n    - ece_raw\n    - ece_tempcal\n    rows:\n    - - 4\n      - 4\n      - 0.612\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.298\n      - 0.192\n    - - 16\n      - 17\n      - 0.653\n      - 0.661\n      - 0.251\n      - 0.148\n    - - 28\n      - 30\n      - 0.694\n      - 0.708\n      - 0.224\n      - 0.121\n    - - 40\n      - 43\n      - 0.721\n      - 0.737\n      - 0.203\n      - 0.098\n    - - 52\n      - 55\n      - 0.742\n      - 0.755\n      - 0.191\n      - 0.082\n    - - 60\n      - 64\n      - 0.758\n      - 0.769\n      - 0.183\n      - 0.075\n    - - 72\n      - 77\n      - 0.776\n      - 0.783\n      - 0.174\n      - 0.069\n    - - 80\n      - 85\n      - 0.771\n      - 0.779\n      - 0.177\n      - 0.072\n    - - 88\n      - 94\n      - 0.753\n      - 0.762\n      - 0.186\n      - 0.079\n    - - 92\n      - 98\n      - 0.728\n      - 0.739\n      - 0.198\n      - 0.091\n  - name: 'Table 2: Qwen3-23