14.0\n842\t      - 0.38\n843\t    - - sw\n844\t      - low\n845\t      - 12\n846\t      - 0.518\n847\t      - 15.0\n848\t      - 0.43\n849\t    - - yo\n850\t      - low\n851\t      - 8\n852\t      - 0.492\n853\t      - 13.9\n854\t      - 0.48\n855\t  - name: 'Table 6: Cross-Architecture Pareto Agreement'\n856\t    columns:\n857\t    - metric\n858\t    - qwen3_235b\n859\t    - llama4_scout\n860\t    - agreement\n861\t    rows:\n862\t    - - global_spearman_rho\n863\t      - -0.38\n864\t      - -0.18\n865\t      - —\n866\t    - - per_lang_rho_median\n867\t      - -0.25\n868\t      - -0.12\n869\t      - —\n870\t    - - sign_consistency\n871\t      - 0.63\n872\t      - 0.53\n873\t      - —\n874\t    - - n_nondominated\n875\t      - 5/24\n876\t      - 3/12\n877\t      - —\n878\t    - - zs_auroc_mean\n879\t      - 0.618\n880\t      - 0.588\n881\t      - —\n882\t    - - optimal_layer_rank_tau\n883\t      - —\n884\t      - —\n885\t      - 0.33\n886\t    - - pass_lang_jaccard\n887\t      - —\n888\t      - —\n889\t      - 0.47\n890\t  figures_description:\n891\t  - 'Figure 2a: Side-by-side Pareto scatter plots. Qwen3 (left) shows a discernible\n892\t    convex front with 5 non-dominated points. Llama 4 (right) shows a near-flat cloud\n893\t    with 3 weakly non-dominated points — no meaningful tradeoff curve. Key Figure\n894\t    2 evidence for architecture dependence.\n895\t\n896\t    '\n897\t  - 'Figure 2b: Llama 4 non-monotonicity detail — line plot of transfer AUROC vs layer\n898\t    depth for zh, fr, ar, sw. Bump at L20, dip at L24, partial recovery at L28 for\n899\t    zh/fr. Attributed to MoE routing transitions.\n900\t\n901\t    '\n902\t  - 'Figure 2c: Per-language Spearman ρ comparison (Qwen3 vs Llama4), paired bar chart.\n903\t    High-resource languages show negative ρ on both (but weaker on Llama4). Low-resource\n904\t    languages show positive ρ on both architectures.\n905\t\n906\t    '\n907\t  analysis: 'Llama 4 Scout demolishes the universal Pareto claim. ρ = −0.18 globally,\n908\t    median per-lang ρ = −0.12, sign consistency 0.53. Cross-architecture agreement\n909\t    τ = 0.33. This transforms the paper: the depth-transfer tradeoff is architecture-dependent,\n910\t    not a universal transformer property. Practitioners cannot use layer selection\n911\t    findings from one model on another. For the diagnostic paper, this is reframed\n912\t    as a key finding: \"the field cannot assume probe-based calibration will behave\n913\t    consistently across architectures.\"\n914\t\n915\t    '\n916\t- name: CaCLR Implementation and Comparative Evaluation\n917\t  plan_id: plan_003\n918\t  description: 'Implement CaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency with MMD). Train\n919\t    using English labels + unlabeled parallel activations from GlobalMMLU train split.\n920\t    Compare vs zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection, few-shot across full Pareto\n921\t    frontier for both models × 19 target languages. α sweep (0.01–10.0), β sweep across\n922\t    3 OOM (0.001–1.0). Fixed-layer results (50% depth + EN-optimal) alongside per-language\n923\t    oracle.\n924\t\n925\t    '\n926\t  expected_results:\n927\t    best_alpha: 0.3\n928\t    best_beta: 0.05\n929\t    beta_sensitivity: flat — β from 0.001 to 1.0 changes AUROC by <0.4pp; consistency\n930\t      loss is inert\n931\t    alpha_sensitivity: moderate — α = 0.1–1.0 gives similar results; inverted-U peaking\n932\t      at 0.3\n933\t    caclr_auroc_mean_qwen3: 0.623\n934\t    caclr_auroc_mean_llama4: 0.594\n935\t    caclr_vs_zs_delta_auroc_mean_pp_qwen3: 0.5\n936\t    caclr_vs_zs_delta_auroc_mean_pp_llama4: 0.6\n937\t    caclr_vs_zs_delta_auroc_median_pp: 0.003\n938\t    caclr_vs_linproj_delta_auroc_mean_pp_qwen3: -1.3\n939\t    caclr_vs_linproj_delta_auroc_mean_pp_llama4: -1.4\n940\t    caclr_vs_fewshot100_delta_pp_qwen3: -3.9\n941\t    caclr_vs_fewshot100_delta_pp_llama4: -4.7\n942\t    caclr_ece_mean_qwen3: 0.186\n943\t    caclr_ece_mean_llama4: 0.201\n944\t    caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pp: -0.002\n945\t    ablation_bce_only_auroc_mean: 0.618\n946\t    ablation_bce_sbece_auroc_mean: 0.627\n947\t    ablation_bce_consistency_auroc_mean: 0.611\n948\t    ablation_full_caclr_auroc_mean: 0.621\n949\t    beta_delta_over_alpha_only_pp: -0.006\n950\t    cohens_d_caclr_vs_zs: 0.04\n951\t    cohens_d_caclr_vs_linproj: -0.18\n952\t    permutation_test_caclr_vs_zs_p: 0.71\n953\t    caclr_per_lang_qwen3:\n954\t      zh:\n955\t        auroc: 0.671\n956\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.3\n957\t      fr:\n958\t        auroc: 0.667\n959\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.5\n960\t      de:\n961\t        auroc: 0.664\n962\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.7\n963\t      es:\n964\t        auroc: 0.657\n965\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.4\n966\t      ja:\n967\t        auroc: 0.653\n968\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.5\n969\t      ko:\n970\t        auroc: 0.652\n971\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.4\n972\t      ru:\n973\t        auroc: 0.649\n974\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.3\n975\t      pl:\n976\t        auroc: 0.631\n977\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.3\n978\t      ar:\n979\t        auroc: 0.631\n980\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.6\n981\t      id:\n982\t        auroc: 0.627\n983\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.6\n984\t      hi:\n985\t        auroc: 0.625\n986\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.7\n987\t      uk:\n988\t        auroc: 0.621\n989\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.7\n990\t      vi:\n991\t        auroc: 0.615\n992\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.8\n993\t      th:\n994\t        auroc: 0.603\n995\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.9\n996\t      bn:\n997\t        auroc: 0.564\n998\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.1\n999\t      te:\n1000\t        auroc: 0.581\n1001\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.0\n1002\t      ta:\n1003\t        auroc: 0.578\n1004\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.0\n1005\t      sw:\n1006\t        auroc: 0.549\n1007\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.2\n1008\t      yo:\n1009\t        auroc: 0.526\n1010\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.4\n1011\t  result_rationale: 'CaCLR fully fails as a method contribution. The consistency loss\n1012\t    (β·L_consistency) is inert: sweeping β over 3 orders of magnitude produces a flat\n1013\t    AUROC landscape (<0.4pp variation). Adding consistency to BCE+SB-ECE actually\n1014\t    hurts by −0.6pp because MMD-based alignment collapses both signal and noise —\n1015\t    the unlabeled parallel activations contain massive language-specific variance\n1016\t    (script, morphology, tokenization artifacts) that dominates the correctness signal.\n1017\t    Minimizing distributional distance between languages forces the probe to ignore\n1018\t    language-specific activation patterns that sometimes carry cross-lingual correctness\n1019\t    information. The ablation reveals that the only beneficial component is SB-ECE\n1020\t    regularization (+0.9pp), which acts as a calibration regularizer on the English\n1021\t    training side but has nothing to do with cross-lingual transfer. Full CaCLR ends\n1022\t    up as \"zero-shot + SB-ECE regularization\" — a trivially achievable benefit that\n1023\t    does not justify the method''s complexity. Linear projection beats CaCLR because\n1024\t    it directly estimates the geometric transformation between language subspaces,\n1025\t    which is more data-efficient than trying to learn an invariant representation\n1026\t    from noisy distributional signals. The paired permutation test CaCLR vs zero-shot\n1027\t    gives p = 0.71 — no significant difference. Cohen''s d = 0.04 confirms negligible\n1028\t    effect size. CaCLR slightly helps low-resource languages (+1pp) and slightly hurts\n1029\t    high-resource (−0.5pp), but both effects are within noise. For the paper, CaCLR\n1030\t    is relegated to one paragraph: \"the natural distributional- matching approach\n1031\t    fails — consistency-based objectives do not help when cross-lingual correctness\n1032\t    SNR is low.\"\n1033\t\n1034\t    '\n1035\t  tables:\n1036\t  - name: 'Table 7: Transfer Method Comparison — Both Models (19-Language Mean)'\n1037\t    columns:\n1038\t    - method\n1039\t    - qwen3_auroc\n1040\t    - qwen3_ece\n1041\t    - llama4_auroc\n1042\t    - llama4_ece\n1043\t    - cohens_d_vs_zs\n1044\t    - labels_needed\n1045\t    rows:\n1046\t    - - zero_shot\n1047\t      - 0.618\n1048\t      - 0.191\n1049\t      - 0.588\n1050\t      - 0.208\n1051\t      - —\n1052\t      - 0 target\n1053\t    - - mean_shift\n1054\t      - 0.618\n1055\t      - 0.207\n1056\t      - 0.588\n1057\t      - 0.224\n1058\t      - 0.0\n1059\t      - 0 target (ΔAUROC≡0)\n1060\t    - - CaCLR\n1061\t      - 0.623\n1062\t      - 0.186\n1063\t      - 0.594\n1064\t      - 0.201\n1065\t      - 0.04\n1066\t      - 0 target + parallel\n1067\t    - - linear_projection\n1068\t      - 0.636\n1069\t      - 0.176\n1070\t      - 0.608\n1071\t      - 0.189\n1072\t      - 0.24\n1073\t      - ~50 parallel\n1074\t    - - few_shot_25\n1075\t      - 0.644\n1076\t      - 0.169\n1077\t      - 0.617\n1078\t      - 0.182\n1079\t      - 0.35\n1080\t      - 25 target\n1081\t    - - few_shot_100\n1082\t      - 0.662\n1083\t      - 0.154\n1084\t      - 0.641\n1085\t      - 0.168\n1086\t      - 0.58\n1087\t      - 100 target\n1088\t  - name: 'Table 8: CaCLR Ablation (Qwen3-235B, 19-Language Mean)'\n1089\t    columns:\n1090\t    - variant\n1091\t    - auroc_mean\n1092\t    - ece_mean\n1093\t    - delta_vs_full_pp\n1094\t    rows:\n1095\t    - - Full CaCLR (BCE+SB-ECE+Consistency)\n1096\t      - 0.621\n1097\t      - 0.186\n1098\t      - 0.0\n1099\t    - - BCE+SB-ECE (no consistency)\n1100\t      - 0.627\n1101\t      - 0.179\n1102\t      - 0.6\n1103\t    - - BCE+Consistency (no SB-ECE)\n1104\t      - 0.611\n1105\t      - 0.198\n1106\t      - -1.0\n1107\t    - - BCE only (= zero-shot probe)\n1108\t      - 0.618\n1109\t      - 0.191\n1110\t      - -0.3\n1111\t    - - Linear Projection (reference)\n1112\t      - 0.636\n1113\t      - 0.176\n1114\t      - 1.5\n1115\t  - name: 'Table 9: CaCLR β Sensitivity (Qwen3, α fixed at 0.3, 19-lang mean AUROC)'\n1116\t    columns:\n1117\t    - beta\n1118\t    - auroc_mean\n1119\t    - delta_vs_beta0_pp\n1120\t    rows:\n1121\t    - - 0.0\n1122\t      - 0.627\n1123\t      - 0.0\n1124\t    - - 0.001\n1125\t      - 0.626\n1126\t      - -0.1\n1127\t    - - 0.005\n1128\t      - 0.625\n1129\t      - -0.2\n1130\t    - - 0.01\n1131\t      - 0.625\n1132\t      - -0.2\n1133\t    - - 0.05\n1134\t      - 0.623\n1135\t      - -0.4\n1136\t    - - 0.1\n1137\t      - 0.621\n1138\t      - -0.6\n1139\t    - - 0.5\n1140\t      - 0.618\n1141\t      - -0.9\n1142\t    - - 1.0\n1143\t      - 0.614\n1144\t      - -1.3\n1145\t  figures_description:\n1146\t  - 'Figure 3a: Grouped bar chart of 6 methods × 19 languages (Qwen3), sorted by resource\n1147\t    tier. Few-shot-100 > few-shot-25 ≈ linproj > CaCLR ≈ zero-shot >> mean-shift (dead).\n1148\t    CaCLR and zero-shot bars nearly identical height everywhere. Visual message: CaCLR\n1149\t    adds complexity for zero benefit.\n1150\t\n1151\t    '\n1152\t  - 'Figure 3b: Hyperparameter sensitivity. Left: AUROC vs α, gentle inverted-U peaking\n1153\t    at 0.3. Right: AUROC vs β (log scale), flat line with slight downward trend —\n1154\t    β is inert at best, harmful at worst. This is the key evidence that consistency\n1155\t    loss fails.\n1156\t\n1157\t    '\n1158\t  - 'Figure 3c: Per-language CaCLR delta (y) vs ZS baseline AUROC (x). Slight positive\n1159\t    slope (CaCLR helps low-resource modestly, hurts high-resource slightly). But all\n1160\t    deltas within ±1.5pp. Linear projection dominates CaCLR at every point.\n1161\t\n1162\t    '\n1163\t  analysis: 'CaCLR is a rigorous negative result. The ablation cleanly isolates the\n1164\t    failure: consistency loss is harmful (adding it to BCE+SB-ECE reduces AUROC by\n1165\t    0.6pp). The β sweep over 3 OOM confirms this is not a tuning failure — the entire\n1166\t    β landscape is flat or declining. CKA analysis (plan_011) provides the mechanistic\n1167\t    explanation: cross-lingual misalignment at deeper layers is geometric (correctness\n1168\t    direction rotates), not distributional (mean/cov shift). MMD-based alignment cannot\n1169\t    correct rotation, which is why linear projection (learns rotation) succeeds where\n1170\t    CaCLR (matches distributions) fails. For the paper, CaCLR is reported as attempted-and-failed,\n1171\t    saving the community from pursuing distributional-matching approaches for this\n1172\t    problem class.\n1173\t\n1174\t    '\n1175\t- name: Difficulty-Controlled Ablation (Decomposition)\n1176\t  plan_id: plan_004\n1177\t  description: 'Train IRT difficulty probes (Ridge regression on human difficulty\n1178\t    scores per Civelli et al.), project out difficulty direction from activations,\n1179\t    retrain correctness probes on residuals, evaluate transfer. 10 languages × 8 layers\n1180\t    per model. Include random-direction control.\n1181\t\n1182\t    '\n1183\t  expected_results:\n1184\t    difficulty_probe_r2_en_qwen3: 0.52\n1185\t    difficulty_probe_r2_en_llama4: 0.47\n1186\t    difficulty_probe_transfer_r2_mean_9lang: 0.38\n1187\t    decomp_difficulty_var_explained_pct_qwen3: 14.3\n1188\t    decomp_difficulty_var_explained_pct_llama4: 11.8\n1189\t    correctness_on_residual_auroc_en_qwen3: 0.741\n1190\t    correctness_on_residual_auroc_en_llama4: 0.714\n1191\t    residual_transfer_auroc_mean_qwen3: 0.613\n1192\t    residual_transfer_auroc_mean_llama4: 0.583\n1193\t    transfer_delta_after_projection_pp_qwen3: -0.5\n1194\t    transfer_delta_after_projection_pp_llama4: -0.5\n1195\t    transfer_delta_range_across_langs_pp:\n1196\t    - -2.1\n1197\t    - 1.3\n1198\t    random_direction_control_delta_pp: -0.3\n1199\t    difficulty_correctness_cosine_mean: 0.36\n1200\t    difficulty_correctness_cosine_std: 0.11\n1201\t    decomp_prescriptive: false\n1202\t    pct_langs_helped_by_projection: 0.3\n1203\t    pct_langs_hurt_by_projection: 0.5\n1204\t    pct_langs_unchanged: 0.2\n1205\t  result_rationale: 'Decomposition is inconclusive. Projecting out difficulty direction\n1206\t    changes transfer AUROC by −0.5pp on average — within noise (random-direction control\n1207\t    shows −0.3pp from dimensionality reduction alone). The per-language pattern shows\n1208\t    no systematic structure: 3/10 languages improve slightly (1–1.3pp), 5/10 worsen\n1209\t    (0.5–2.1pp), 2/10 unchanged. The difficulty direction explains only 14% of correctness\n1210\t    variance (Qwen3), meaning the decomposition captures a real but minor factor.\n1211\t    Critically, the difficulty-shared component turns out to carry useful cross-lingual\n1212\t    signal: questions that are hard in English tend to be hard everywhere, and this\n1213\t    shared difficulty is one of few reliable cross-lingual bridges. Removing it strips\n1214\t    away a genuine invariance. The residual \"capability-specific\" component is more\n1215\t    language-dependent, not less — containing idiosyncratic encoding patterns. The\n1216\t    cosine between difficulty and correctness directions (0.36 ± 0.11) confirms they\n1217\t    are neither orthogonal nor aligned — the moderate overlap prevents clean separation.\n1218\t    This contradicts Claim 2''s hypothesis that decomposition would be prescriptive.\n1219\t    For the paper, decomposition is reported as \"difficulty and capability are entangled\n1220\t    in multilingual representations, unlike the clean separation assumed by English-only\n1221\t    work.\"\n1222\t\n1223\t    '\n1224\t  tables:\n1225\t  - name: 'Table 10: Difficulty Probe Quality (Ridge, R² on Held-Out)'\n1226\t    columns:\n1227\t    - language\n1228\t    - resource_tier\n1229\t    - qwen3_r2\n1230\t    - llama4_r2\n1231\t    rows:\n1232\t    - - en\n1233\t      - —\n1234\t      - 0.52\n1235\t      - 0.47\n1236\t    - - zh\n1237\t      - high\n1238\t      - 0.48\n1239\t      - 0.43\n1240\t    - - fr\n1241\t      - high\n1242\t      - 0.47\n1243\t      - 0.42\n1244\t    - - de\n1245\t      - high\n1246\t      - 0.46\n1247\t      - 0.41\n1248\t    - - hi\n1249\t      - mid\n1250\t      - 0.41\n1251\t      - 0.36\n1252\t    - - ru\n1253\t      - mid\n1254\t      - 0.43\n1255\t      - 0.38\n1256\t    - - ko\n1257\t      - mid\n1258\t      - 0.42\n1259\t      - 0.37\n1260\t    - - tr\n1261\t      - mid\n1262\t      - 0.39\n1263\t      - 0.34\n1264\t    - - sw\n1265\t      - low\n1266\t      - 0.3\n1267\t      - 0.25\n1268\t    - - bn\n1269\t      - low\n1270\t      - 0.28\n1271\t      - 0.23\n1272\t  - name: 'Table 11: Decomposition Transfer Ablation (9-Language Mean)'\n1273\t    columns:\n1274\t    - condition\n1275\t    - qwen3_auroc\n1276\t    - qwen3_delta_pp\n1277\t    - llama4_auroc\n1278\t    - llama4_delta_pp\n1279\t    rows:\n1280\t    - - Full activations (baseline)\n1281\t      - 0.618\n1282\t      - 0.0\n1283\t      - 0.588\n1284\t      - 0.0\n1285\t    - - Residual (difficulty out)\n1286\t      - 0.613\n1287\t      - -0.5\n1288\t      - 0.583\n1289\t      - -0.5\n1290\t    - - Difficulty component only\n1291\t      - 0.569\n1292\t      - -4.9\n1293\t      - 0.548\n1294\t      - -4.0\n1295\t    - - Random direction out (ctrl)\n1296\t      - 0.615\n1297\t      - -0.3\n1298\t      - 0.585\n1299\t      - -0.3\n1300\t  - name: 'Table 12: Per-Language Transfer Delta After Difficulty Projection (Qwen3)'\n1301\t    columns:\n1302\t    - language\n1303\t    - resource_tier\n1304\t    - delta_auroc_pp\n1305\t    - direction\n1306\t    rows:\n1307\t    - - zh\n1308\t      - high\n1309\t      - -0.8\n1310\t      - hurt\n1311\t    - - fr\n1312\t      - high\n1313\t      - -1.2\n1314\t      - hurt\n1315\t    - - de\n1316\t      - high\n1317\t      - -0.6\n1318\t      - hurt\n1319\t    - - hi\n1320\t      - mid\n1321\t      - 0.4\n1322\t      - helped\n1323\t    - - ru\n1324\t      - mid\n1325\t      - -0.3\n1326\t      - unchanged\n1327\t    - - ko\n1328\t      - mid\n1329\t      - 1.0\n1330\t      - helped\n1331\t    - - tr\n1332\t      - mid\n1333\t      - -2.1\n1334\t      - hurt\n1335\t    - - sw\n1336\t      - low\n1337\t      - 1.3\n1338\t      - helped\n1339\t    - - bn\n1340\t      - low\n1341\t      - -0.8\n1342\t      - hurt\n1343\t  figures_description:\n1344\t  - 'Figure 4a: Scatter of per-language transfer delta after projection (y) vs baseline\n1345\t    ZS AUROC (x). Points scatter around zero with no clear pattern — helps some, hurts\n1346\t    others, irrespective of resource tier. The random-direction control mean (dashed\n1347\t    line at −0.3pp) shows the projection delta is barely distinguishable from dimensionality-reduction\n1348\t    noise.\n1349\t\n1350\t    '\n1351\t  - 'Figure 4b: Principal angle histogram between difficulty and correctness subspaces.\n1352\t    Peak at ~55–60° with wide spread (std ~11°) across layers and languages. Far from\n1353\t    both 0° and 90° — the relationship is inconsistent, preventing clean decomposition.\n1354\t\n1355\t    '\n1356\t  analysis: 'Decomposition fails as a prescriptive tool. The difficulty-shared component\n1357\t    is load-bearing for cross-lingual transfer — removing it hurts more often than\n1358\t    it helps (5/10 vs 3/10), and the average delta (−0.5pp) is barely above the random-direction\n1359\t    control (−0.3pp). The original Claim 2 hypothesis is abandoned. For the diagnostic\n1360\t    paper, this becomes: \"difficulty and capability are entangled in multilingual\n1361\t    representations — the clean separation reported in English-only work does not\n1362\t    generalize cross-lingually.\"\n1363\t\n1364\t    '\n1365\t- name: Cross-Lingual Orthogonality Analysis\n1366\t  plan_id: plan_005\n1367\t  description: 'Extract verbalized confidence direction (prompt model for confidence\n1368\t    score, fit probe), compute cosine similarity with correctness direction across\n1369\t    20 languages × 2 architectures. Analyze by resource tier and language family.\n1370\t\n1371\t    '\n1372\t  expected_results:\n1373\t    orthogonality_cosine_mean_overall: 0.074\n1374\t    orthogonality_cosine_std_overall: 0.036\n1375\t    orthogonality_cosine_mean_qwen3: 0.069\n1376\t    orthogonality_cosine_mean_llama4: 0.079\n1377\t    orthogonality_cosine_max_across_all: 0.172\n1378\t    orthogonality_cosine_high_resource: 0.052\n1379\t    orthogonality_cosine_mid_resource: 0.073\n1380\t    orthogonality_cosine_low_resource: 0.108\n1381\t    pct_langs_cosine_below_0p10: 0.73\n1382\t    pct_langs_cosine_below_0p05: 0.3\n1383\t    outlier_langs_above_0p15:\n1384\t    - yo_llama4\n1385\t    confidence_probe_r2_en: 0.71\n1386\t    confidence_probe_transfer_r2_mean: 0.58\n1387\t    cosine_resource_tier_spearman: -0.61\n1388\t    per_lang_cosine:\n1389\t      en:\n1390\t        qwen3: 0.036\n1391\t        llama4: 0.041\n1392\t        mean: 0.039\n1393\t      zh:\n1394\t        qwen3: 0.041\n1395\t        llama4: 0.048\n1396\t        mean: 0.045\n1397\t      fr:\n1398\t        qwen3: 0.044\n1399\t        llama4: 0.052\n1400\t        mean: 0.048\n1401\t      de:\n1402\t        qwen3: 0.046\n1403\t        llama4: 0.054\n1404\t        mean: 0.05\n1405\t      es:\n1406\t        qwen3: 0.048\n1407\t        llama4: 0.057\n1408\t        mean: 0.053\n1409\t      ja:\n1410\t        qwen3: 0.053\n1411\t        llama4: 0.061\n1412\t        mean: 0.057\n1413\t      ru:\n1414\t        qwen3: 0.056\n1415\t        llama4: 0.064\n1416\t        mean: 0.06\n1417\t      ko:\n1418\t        qwen3: 0.058\n1419\t        llama4: 0.067\n1420\t        mean: 0.063\n1421\t      ar:\n1422\t        qwen3: 0.064\n1423\t        llama4: 0.073\n1424\t        mean: 0.069\n1425\t      hi:\n1426\t        qwen3: 0.067\n1427\t        llama4: 0.076\n1428\t        mean: 0.072\n1429\t      vi:\n1430\t        qwen3: 0.069\n1431\t        llama4: 0.078\n1432\t        mean: 0.074\n1433\t      id:\n1434\t        qwen3: 0.065\n1435\t        llama4: 0.074\n1436\t        mean: 0.07\n1437\t      th:\n1438\t        qwen3: 0.074\n1439\t        llama4: 0.084\n1440\t        mean: 0.079\n1441\t      pl:\n1442\t        qwen3: 0.06\n1443\t        llama4: 0.068\n1444\t        mean: 0.064\n1445\t      uk:\n1446\t        qwen3: 0.063\n1447\t        llama4: 0.072\n1448\t        mean: 0.068\n1449\t      bn:\n1450\t        qwen3: 0.085\n1451\t        llama4: 0.098\n1452\t        mean: 0.092\n1453\t      te:\n1454\t        qwen3: 0.092\n1455\t        llama4: 0.106\n1456\t        mean: 0.099\n1457\t      ta:\n1458\t        qwen3: 0.096\n1459\t        llama4: 0.112\n1460\t        mean: 0.104\n1461\t      sw:\n1462\t        qwen3: 0.104\n1463\t        llama4: 0.124\n1464\t        mean: 0.114\n1465\t      yo:\n1466\t        qwen3: 0.121\n1467\t        llama4: 0.172\n1468\t        mean: 0.147\n1469\t  result_rationale: 'Orthogonality persists cross-lingually (mean cosine 0.074) because\n1470\t    it reflects a structural property of how LLMs separate verbalized confidence from\n1471\t    actual correctness at the representation level. However, the worst-tier variance\n1472\t    is higher than Miao & Ungar''s English-only finding (std 0.036 vs ~0.01): low-resource\n1473\t    languages show weaker orthogonality (cosine ~0.108) because the model has less\n1474\t    capacity to maintain the confidence-correctness dissociation for languages with\n1475\t    limited training data. Yoruba on Llama4 reaches 0.172 — an outlier above the 0.15\n1476\t    threshold that weakens the universality claim. This happens because Llama4 has\n1477\t    minimal Yoruba training data, so both correctness and confidence signals degrade\n1478\t    in correlated ways, partially breaking orthogonality. The resource-tier gradient\n1479\t    (high: 0.052, mid: 0.073, low: 0.108, Spearman ρ = −0.61 with resource level)\n1480\t    is the novel angle, but effect size is modest. Reviewers may see the extension\n1481\t    from English-only (Miao & Ungar) to 20 languages as incremental rather than novel,\n1482\t    since the qualitative finding is the same.\n1483\t\n1484\t    '\n1485\t  tables:\n1486\t  - name: 'Table 13: Correctness-Confidence Cosine Similarity by Resource Tier'\n1487\t    columns:\n1488\t    - resource_tier\n1489\t    - n_langs\n1490\t    - qwen3_mean\n1491\t    - llama4_mean\n1492\t    - overall_mean\n1493\t    - std\n1494\t    rows:\n1495\t    - - high\n1496\t      - 8\n1497\t      - 0.048\n1498\t      - 0.056\n1499\t      - 0.052\n1500\t      - 0.008\n1501\t    - - mid\n1502\t      - 7\n1503\t      - 0.066\n1504\t      - 0.075\n1505\t      - 0.073\n1506\t      - 0.005\n1507\t    - - low\n1508\t      - 5\n1509\t      - 0.1\n1510\t      - 0.122\n1511\t      - 0.108\n1512\t      - 0.02\n1513\t    - - all\n1514\t      - 20\n1515\t      - 0.069\n1516\t      - 0.079\n1517\t      - 0.074\n1518\t      - 0.036\n1519\t  figures_description:\n1520\t  - 'Figure 5a: Bar chart of cosine similarity for 20 languages, sorted by resource\n1521\t    tier. Miao & Ungar''s English threshold (0.04) as dashed line. Most high-resource\n1522\t    near the line; low-resource 2–3× higher. Yoruba (0.172 on Llama4) breaches 0.15,\n1523\t    marked with asterisk.\n1524\t\n1525\t    '\n1526\t  - 'Figure 5b: Scatter of cosine (y) vs log resource size (x), colored by architecture.\n1527\t    Moderate negative correlation. Yoruba is the visible outlier pulling the trend.\n1528\t\n1529\t    '\n1530\t  analysis: 'Orthogonality survives as the one mechanistic takeaway: output-based\n1531\t    calibration is structurally limited everywhere, not just in English. But the contribution\n1532\t    is incremental — same qualitative finding as Miao & Ungar, extended to 20 languages.\n1533\t    The resource-tier modulation is novel (effect size 0.052 vs 0.108) but the Yoruba\n1534\t    outlier at 0.172 weakens the universality claim. For the paper: \"correctness-confidence\n1535\t    dissociation is multilingual, confirming output-based calibration faces a structural\n1536\t    ceiling regardless of language, but this ceiling is noisier for low-resource languages.\"\n1537\t\n1538\t    '\n1539\t- name: Probe Complexity Comparison\n1540\t  plan_id: plan_006\n1541\t  description: 'Logistic regression vs 2-layer MLP at each probed layer for both models.\n1542\t    Compare English accuracy, cross-lingual transfer, calibration. Test hypothesis:\n1543\t    linear probes transfer better.\n1544\t\n1545\t    '\n1546\t  expected_results:\n1547\t    mlp_vs_logreg_en_auroc_delta_mean_pp: 1.0\n1548\t    mlp_vs_logreg_transfer_auroc_delta_mean_pp: -0.3\n1549\t    hypothesis_linear_transfers_better: weak support\n1550\t    cohens_d_transfer_difference: 0.15\n1551\t    permutation_test_p: 0.34\n1552\t    mlp_en_advantage_deep_layers_pp: 1.4\n1553\t    logreg_transfer_advantage_deep_layers_pp: 0.6\n1554\t    interaction_depth_probe_significant: false\n1555\t  result_rationale: 'Linear probes transfer marginally better (−0.3pp MLP penalty\n1556\t    in transfer vs +1.0pp MLP advantage in English), but Cohen''s d = 0.15 and the\n1557\t    permutation test is non-significant (p = 0.34). The effect is too small to be\n1558\t    practically meaningful. MLP captures mild nonlinearities in the correctness signal\n1559\t    but these are English-specific features that don''t generalize. The depth interaction\n1560\t    exists weakly (MLP overfits more at deep layers) but isn''t statistically significant\n1561\t    as an interaction term. This validates logistic regression as the right default\n1562\t    but generates no standalone finding.\n1563\t\n1564\t    '\n1565\t  tables:\n1566\t  - name: 'Table 14: Probe Complexity Comparison (Mean Across All Layers)'\n1567\t    columns:\n1568\t    - model\n1569\t    - probe\n1570\t    - en_auroc_mean\n1571\t    - transfer_auroc_mean\n1572\t    - en_ece_mean\n1573\t    - transfer_ece_mean\n1574\t    rows:\n1575\t    - - qwen3\n1576\t      - logreg\n1577\t      - 0.714\n1578\t      - 0.618\n1579\t      - 0.206\n1580\t      - 0.191\n1581\t    - - qwen3\n1582\t      - mlp\n1583\t      - 0.725\n1584\t      - 0.615\n1585\t      - 0.198\n1586\t      - 0.194\n1587\t    - - llama4\n1588\t      - logreg\n1589\t      - 0.697\n1590\t      - 0.588\n1591\t      - 0.219\n1592\t      - 0.208\n1593\t    - - llama4\n1594\t      - mlp\n1595\t      - 0.706\n1596\t      - 0.586\n1597\t      - 0.212\n1598\t      - 0.211\n1599\t  figures_description:\n1600\t  - 'Figure S1 (supp): Paired difference plot (MLP − LogReg AUROC) vs layer depth.\n1601\t    English panel: positive, increasing with depth. Transfer panel: near-zero, slightly\n1602\t    negative. Bootstrap CIs overlap zero at most layers for transfer.\n1603\t\n1604\t    '\n1605\t  analysis: 'Non-finding. Effect is real but negligible (d = 0.15). Logistic regression\n1606\t    confirmed as default choice: simpler, nearly identical performance, better interpretability.\n1607\t    Goes in supplementary as methods validation.\n1608\t\n1609\t    '\n1610\t- name: Additional Benchmark Evaluation (Belebele + MMLU-ProX)\n1611\t  plan_id: plan_007\n1612\t  description: 'Evaluate best probes + CaCLR on Belebele (reading comprehension, 20\n1613\t    languages) and MMLU-ProX (translated MMLU, 20 languages). Tests cross-benchmark\n1614\t    generalization.\n1615\t\n1616\t    '\n1617\t  expected_results:\n1618\t    belebele_en_auroc_qwen3: 0.741\n1619\t    belebele_en_auroc_llama4: 0.718\n1620\t    belebele_zs_transfer_mean_qwen3: 0.594\n1621\t    belebele_zs_transfer_mean_llama4: 0.567\n1622\t    belebele_drop_vs_globalmmlu_pp: 2.4\n1623\t    belebele_caclr_delta_vs_linproj_pp: -0.8\n1624\t    mmlupx_en_auroc_qwen3: 0.762\n1625\t    mmlupx_en_auroc_llama4: 0.738\n1626\t    mmlupx_zs_transfer_mean_qwen3: 0.579\n1627\t    mmlupx_zs_transfer_mean_llama4: 0.551\n1628\t    mmlupx_drop_vs_globalmmlu_pp: 3.9\n1629\t    mmlupx_caclr_delta_vs_linproj_pp: -1.1\n1630\t    lang_rank_corr_globalmmlu_belebele: 0.83\n1631\t    lang_rank_corr_globalmmlu_mmlupx: 0.69\n1632\t  result_rationale: 'Belebele partially replicates because it is high-quality and\n1633\t    natively multilingual. The 2.4pp transfer drop reflects domain shift (reading\n1634\t    comprehension vs knowledge). MMLU-ProX shows worse transfer (3.9pp drop) because\n1635\t    machine-translated MMLU introduces systematic distributional shifts (ambiguous\n1636\t    domain terms, cultural references, low-resource translation errors). CaCLR remains\n1637\t    worse than linear projection on both benchmarks, confirming the negative result\n1638\t    generalizes. Language ranking is stable for Belebele (ρ = 0.83) but degrades for\n1639\t    MMLU-ProX (ρ = 0.69) due to translation quality variation.\n1640\t\n1641\t    '\n1642\t  tables:\n1643\t  - name: 'Table 15: Cross-Benchmark Generalization (19-Language Mean)'\n1644\t    columns:\n1645\t    - benchmark\n1646\t    - zs_auroc_qwen3\n1647\t    - zs_auroc_llama4\n1648\t    - caclr_vs_linproj_pp\n1649\t    - lang_rank_corr\n1650\t    rows:\n1651\t    - - GlobalMMLU\n1652\t      - 0.618\n1653\t      - 0.588\n1654\t      - -1.3\n1655\t      - —\n1656\t    - - Belebele\n1657\t      - 0.594\n1658\t      - 0.567\n1659\t      - -0.8\n1660\t      - 0.83\n1661\t    - - MMLU-ProX\n1662\t      - 0.579\n1663\t      - 0.551\n1664\t      - -1.1\n1665\t      - 0.69\n1666\t  figures_description:\n1667\t  - 'Figure S2 (supp): Three-panel scatter showing per-language AUROC correlation\n1668\t    across benchmark pairs. GlobalMMLU–Belebele tight; GlobalMMLU–MMLU-ProX noisier.\n1669\t    Low-resource languages (red) are outliers on MMLU-ProX.\n1670\t\n1671\t    '\n1672\t  analysis: 'Findings partially generalize: Belebele confirms, MMLU-ProX is noisier.\n1673\t    Language ranking stable but absolute performance degrades with domain shift. CaCLR''s\n1674\t    negative result is robust across all three benchmarks. Include Belebele in main\n1675\t    results, MMLU-ProX in supplementary with translation-artifact caveat.\n1676\t\n1677\t    '\n1678\t- name: INT8 Validation Ablation\n1679\t  plan_id: plan_010\n1680\t  description: 'Compare INT8 vs FP16 probing results at 5 representative layers for\n1681\t    Llama 4 Scout on 5% random subset. Validates quantization doesn''t distort correctness\n1682\t    signals.\n1683\t\n1684\t    '\n1685\t  expected_results:\n1686\t    int8_vs_fp16_auroc_delta_mean_pp: -0.3\n1687\t    int8_vs_fp16_auroc_delta_max_pp: -0.7\n1688\t    int8_vs_fp16_ece_delta_mean_pp: 0.2\n1689\t    int8_vs_fp16_activation_cosine_sim_mean: 0.994\n1690\t    int8_vs_fp16_rank_correlation_layers: 0.98\n1691\t    validation_passes: true\n1692\t  result_rationale: 'INT8 quantization introduces minimal distortion. The 0.3pp mean\n1693\t    AUROC difference is within noise — INT8 preserves directional structure of activations\n1694\t    that linear probes rely on. Cosine similarity 0.994 between INT8 and FP16 activations\n1695\t    confirms near-perfect directional alignment. Layer ranking is preserved (rank\n1696\t    corr 0.98). This passes cleanly even in the worst tier — quantization is not a\n1697\t    source of failure.\n1698\t\n1699\t    '\n1700\t  tables:\n1701\t  - name: 'Table S1: INT8 vs FP16 Comparison (Llama 4 Scout, 5% subset)'\n1702\t    columns:\n1703\t    - layer\n1704\t    - fp16_auroc\n1705\t    - int8_auroc\n1706\t    - delta_pp\n1707\t    - activation_cosine\n1708\t    rows:\n1709\t    - - 8\n1710\t      - 0.637\n1711\t      - 0.633\n1712\t      - -0.4\n1713\t      - 0.993\n1714\t    - - 16\n1715\t      - 0.7\n1716\t      - 0.697\n1717\t      - -0.3\n1718\t      - 0.994\n1719\t    - - 24\n1720\t      - 0.731\n1721\t      - 0.729\n1722\t      - -0.2\n1723\t      - 0.995\n1724\t    - - 32\n1725\t      - 0.746\n1726\t      - 0.743\n1727\t      - -0.3\n1728\t      - 0.994\n1729\t    - - 40\n1730\t      - 0.748\n1731\t      - 0.741\n1732\t      - -0.7\n1733\t      - 0.992\n1734\t  figures_description:\n1735\t  - 'Figure S3 (supp): Paired scatter of FP16 vs INT8 AUROC at all layer × language\n1736\t    combinations. Points on y=x diagonal with slight downward bias. Inset: delta histogram\n1737\t    centered near −0.3pp.\n1738\t\n1739\t    '\n1740\t  analysis: 'INT8 validation passes. Quantization is not a confound. One paragraph\n1741\t    + Table S1 in supplementary.\n1742\t\n1743\t    '\n1744\t- name: CKA Cross-Lingual Representation Similarity Analysis\n1745\t  plan_id: plan_011\n1746\t  description: 'Compute CKA (linear kernel) between English and each target language\n1747\t    at every probed layer for both models. Correlate CKA with transfer AUROC to provide\n1748\t    mechanistic explanation for the Pareto frontier. Full-scale validation of pilot\n1749\t    finding (revised hypothesis: threshold model — transfer collapses only below critical\n1750\t    CKA threshold ~0.35). Report CKA × layer × language heatmaps, CKA vs transfer\n1751\t    degradation correlation per language, and threshold analysis.\n1752\t\n1753\t    '\n1754\t  expected_results:\n1755\t    cka_en_vs_target_mean_all: 0.72\n1756\t    cka_decline_with_depth: true\n1757\t    cka_shallow_mean: 0.89\n1758\t    cka_deep_mean: 0.54\n1759\t    cka_vs_transfer_auroc_pearson_r_overall: 0.32\n1760\t    cka_vs_transfer_auroc_pearson_r_range_per_lang:\n1761\t    - -0.08\n1762\t    - 0.71\n1763\t    cka_vs_transfer_pearson_significant_langs: 7\n1764\t    cka_vs_transfer_pearson_nonsig_langs: 12\n1765\t    cka_threshold_0p35_replicates: partial\n1766\t    n_lang_layer_pairs_below_threshold: 18\n1767\t    transfer_collapse_below_threshold_pct: 0.61\n1768\t    transfer_collapse_above_threshold_pct: 0.22\n1769\t    cka_architecture_consistency: weak\n1770\t    cka_pearson_qwen3: 0.38\n1771\t    cka_pearson_llama4: 0.24\n1772\t    cka_by_tier:\n1773\t      high_resource_mean: 0.76\n1774\t      mid_resource_mean: 0.71\n1775\t      low_resource_mean: 0.64\n1776\t    cka_per_lang_deep_layer:\n1777\t      zh: 0.68\n1778\t      fr: 0.71\n1779\t      de: 0.69\n1780\t      es: 0.67\n1781\t      ja: 0.62\n1782\t      ko: 0.6\n1783\t      ru: 0.58\n1784\t      ar: 0.53\n1785\t      hi: 0.51\n1786\t      vi: 0.48\n1787\t      id: 0.52\n1788\t      th: 0.45\n1789\t      pl: 0.55\n1790\t      uk: 0.54\n1791\t      bn: 0.41\n1792\t      te: 0.39\n1793\t      ta: 0.37\n1794\t      sw: 0.33\n1795\t      yo: 0.28\n1796\t    pilot_revision_validated: partially — threshold exists but is noisy\n1797\t  result_rationale: 'CKA declines with depth on both models (shallow 0.89 → deep 0.54),\n1798\t    confirming that deeper layers are less cross-lingually aligned. However, the correlation\n1799\t    between CKA and transfer AUROC is weak overall (Pearson r = 0.32, explaining ~10%\n1800\t    of variance). This happens because the pilot''s revised hypothesis — that transfer\n1801\t    collapses only below a critical CKA threshold (~0.35) rather than declining linearly\n1802\t    — partially replicates but with too much noise at full scale. Below the 0.35 threshold,\n1803\t    61% of language-layer pairs show transfer collapse (AUROC < 0.55), but 39% do\n1804\t    not — meaning the threshold is suggestive, not deterministic. Above the threshold,\n1805\t    22% of pairs still show poor transfer, indicating CKA captures necessary but not\n1806\t    sufficient conditions for transfer. Per-language correlations vary wildly: hi\n1807\t    (r = 0.71, sig) and sw (r = 0.67, sig) show strong CKA-transfer coupling, but\n1808\t    zh (r = 0.12, ns) and fr (r = 0.08, ns) show near-zero correlation because these\n1809\t    high-resource languages transfer well regardless of CKA level. Architecture inconsistency\n1810\t    is notable: Qwen3 r = 0.38, Llama4 r = 0.24 — the mechanistic explanation is model-dependent.\n1811\t    For the paper, CKA provides a \"suggestive but not convincing\" mechanistic account:\n1812\t    geometric misalignment (lower CKA) correlates with transfer failure, but the relationship\n1813\t    is too noisy and architecture-dependent to serve as a reliable predictor or principled\n1814\t    layer selection criterion.\n1815\t\n1816\t    '\n1817\t  tables:\n1818\t  - name: 'Table 16: CKA vs Transfer AUROC Correlation by Architecture'\n1819\t    columns:\n1820\t    - model\n1821\t    - pearson_r\n1822\t    - p_value\n1823\t    - n_sig_langs\n1824\t    - n_total_langs\n1825\t    - spearman_rho\n1826\t    rows:\n1827\t    - - Qwen3-235B\n1828\t      - 0.38\n1829\t      - 0.002\n1830\t      - 5\n1831\t      - 19\n1832\t      - 0.35\n1833\t    - - Llama4-Scout\n1834\t      - 0.24\n1835\t      - 0.041\n1836\t      - 2\n1837\t      - 19\n1838\t      - 0.21\n1839\t    - - Combined\n1840\t      - 0.32\n1841\t      - 0.001\n1842\t      - 7\n1843\t      - 38\n1844\t      - 0.29\n1845\t  - name: 'Table 17: CKA Threshold Analysis (threshold = 0.35)'\n1846\t    columns:\n1847\t    - condition\n1848\t    - n_pairs\n1849\t    - mean_transfer_auroc\n1850\t    - pct_below_0p55\n1851\t    - pct_above_0p65\n1852\t    rows:\n1853\t    - - CKA < 0.35\n1854\t      - 18\n1855\t      - 0.538\n1856\t      - 0.61\n1857\t      - 0.11\n1858\t    - - CKA ≥ 0.35\n1859\t      - 438\n1860\t      - 0.612\n1861\t      - 0.22\n1862\t      - 0.39\n1863\t  - name: 'Table 18: CKA at Deep Layers (75% depth) by Language'\n1864\t    columns:\n1865\t    - language\n1866\t    - resource_tier\n1867\t    - cka_qwen3\n1868\t    - cka_llama4\n1869\t    - transfer_auroc_qwen3\n1870\t    - transfer_auroc_llama4\n1871\t    rows:\n1872\t    - - zh\n1873\t      - high\n1874\t      - 0.71\n1875\t      - 0.65\n1876\t      - 0.668\n1877\t      - 0.658\n1878\t    - - fr\n1879\t      - high\n1880\t      - 0.74\n1881\t      - 0.68\n1882\t      - 0.672\n1883\t      - 0.662\n1884\t    - - de\n1885\t      - high\n1886\t      - 0.72\n1887\t      - 0.66\n1888\t      - 0.671\n1889\t      - 0.655\n1890\t    - - ja\n1891\t      - high\n1892\t      - 0.65\n1893\t      - 0.59\n1894\t      - 0.658\n1895\t      - 0.634\n1896\t    - - ru\n1897\t      - high\n1898\t      - 0.61\n1899\t      - 0.55\n1900\t      - 0.652\n1901\t      - 0.622\n1902\t    - - ar\n1903\t      - mid\n1904\t      - 0.56\n1905\t      - 0.5\n1906\t      - 0.625\n1907\t      - 0.589\n1908\t    - - hi\n1909\t      - mid\n1910\t      - 0.54\n1911\t      - 0.48\n1912\t      - 0.618\n1913\t      - 0.586\n1914\t    - - th\n1915\t      - mid\n1916\t      - 0.48\n1917\t      - 0.42\n1918\t      - 0.594\n1919\t      - 0.558\n1920\t    - - sw\n1921\t      - low\n1922\t      - 0.36\n1923\t      - 0.3\n1924\t      - 0.537\n1925\t      - 0.518\n1926\t    - - yo\n1927\t      - low\n1928\t      - 0.31\n1929\t      - 0.25\n1930\t      - 0.512\n1931\t      - 0.492\n1932\t  figures_description:\n1933\t  - 'Figure 6a: CKA heatmap (y: layers, x: languages sorted by resource tier) for\n1934\t    Qwen3. Clear gradient: top-left (shallow × high-resource) is hot (CKA > 0.85);\n1935\t    bottom-right (deep × low-resource) is cool (CKA < 0.40). But the mid-region is\n1936\t    noisy — no clean contour separating transfer success from failure.\n1937\t\n1938\t    '\n1939\t  - 'Figure 6b: Scatter of CKA (x) vs transfer AUROC (y), one point per language-layer\n1940\t    pair, colored by model. Weak positive trend (r = 0.32). Vertical dashed line at\n1941\t    CKA = 0.35 (pilot threshold). Below threshold: most points cluster low-AUROC,\n1942\t    but some outliers transfer well. Above threshold: wide spread. The scatter is\n1943\t    too noisy for CKA to serve as a reliable predictor.\n1944\t\n1945\t    '\n1946\t  - 'Figure 6c: Per-language CKA-transfer correlation bars, sorted by magnitude. Hi\n1947\t    and sw show strong correlations (r > 0.6); zh and fr near zero. Architecture-colored\n1948\t    bars show Qwen3 correlations consistently higher than Llama4.\n1949\t\n1950\t    '\n1951\t  analysis: 'CKA provides suggestive but not convincing mechanistic explanation. The\n1952\t    decline with depth is clear, and the threshold model partially replicates (61%\n1953\t    transfer collapse below CKA 0.35). But the correlation is too weak (r = 0.32)\n1954\t    and architecture-dependent (0.38 vs 0.24) to serve as a reliable predictor. The\n1955\t    key insight survives at a qualitative level: cross-lingual misalignment is geometric,\n1956\t    which explains why MMD-based CaCLR fails (it matches distributions but can''t\n1957\t    correct rotation) while linear projection succeeds (it learns the rotation). But\n1958\t    the quantitative story is messy. For the paper: report CKA as supporting evidence\n1959\t    for the geometric-misalignment interpretation, not as a standalone finding. Two\n1960\t    paragraphs in the analysis section.\n1961\t\n1962\t    '\n1963\t- name: Linear Projection Sensitivity Analysis\n1964\t  plan_id: plan_012\n1965\t  description: 'Vary number of parallel pairs (10, 20, 50, 100, 200) for linear projection\n1966\t    transfer. Determines minimum data requirement for this baseline. Both models ×\n1967\t    5 representative layers × 19 target languages.\n1968\t\n1969\t    '\n1970\t  expected_results:\n1971\t    linproj_by_n_pairs:\n1972\t      10:\n1973\t        auroc_mean: 0.608\n1974\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: -1.0\n1975\t        pct_langs_improved: 0.26\n1976\t      20:\n1977\t        auroc_mean: 0.621\n1978\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.3\n1979\t        pct_langs_improved: 0.42\n1980\t      50:\n1981\t        auroc_mean: 0.636\n1982\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.8\n1983\t        pct_langs_improved: 0.55\n1984\t      100:\n1985\t        auroc_mean: 0.643\n1986\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 2.5\n1987\t        pct_langs_improved: 0.63\n1988\t      200:\n1989\t        auroc_mean: 0.647\n1990\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 2.9\n1991\t        pct_langs_improved: 0.68\n1992\t    minimum_viable_pairs_high_resource: 20\n1993\t    minimum_viable_pairs_mid_resource: 50\n1994\t    minimum_viable_pairs_low_resource: '>200 (insufficient quality)'\n1995\t    saturation_point: ~100 pairs — diminishing returns beyond\n1996\t    linproj_fails_low_resource: true\n1997\t    low_resource_at_200_pairs:\n1998\t      bn:\n1999\t        auroc: 0.561\n2000\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.8\n2001\t      te:\n2002\t        auroc: 0.576\n2003\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.5\n2004\t      ta:\n2005\t        auroc: 0.571\n2006\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.3\n2007\t      sw:\n2008\t        auroc: 0.543\n2009\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.6\n2010\t      yo:\n2011\t        auroc: 0.518\n2012\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.6\n2013\t    high_resource_at_50_pairs:\n2014\t      zh:\n2015\t        auroc: 0.681\n2016\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.3\n2017\t      fr:\n2018\t        auroc: 0.686\n2019\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.4\n2020\t      de:\n2021\t        auroc: 0.684\n2022\t        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.3\n2023\t    variance_across_random_subsets_std_pp: 1.8\n2024\t  result_rationale: 'Linear projection''s effectiveness depends heavily on the number\n2025\t    and quality of parallel pairs. At 10 pairs, the affine transformation is underestimated\n2026\t    (high-dimensional activation space needs more samples to estimate rotation matrix\n2027\t    reliably), actually hurting transfer (−1.0pp) by introducing noise. At 20 pairs,\n2028\t    high-resource languages start benefiting because their parallel pairs have high\n2029\t    translation quality and the activation subspaces are already partially aligned.\n2030\t    At 50 pairs, the projection stabilizes for high and mid-resource languages (the\n2031\t    \"default\" setting in the main experiments). But low-resource languages fail even\n2032\t    at 200 pairs for two reasons: (1) GlobalMMLU parallel translations for sw/yo/bn\n2033\t    are lower quality (more literal, less natural), so the parallel activations don''t\n2034\t    capture the true language-specific encoding; (2) the activation subspace for these\n2035\t    languages is itself noisier (less structured due to limited pretraining), making\n2036\t    the rotation hard to estimate even with many pairs. The variance across random\n2037\t    subsets (std 1.8pp) is high, indicating the projection is sensitive to which specific\n2038\t    pairs are used — this is a practical reliability concern. Saturation at ~100 pairs\n2039\t    means the projection captures most learnable alignment, and remaining transfer\n2040\t    gap is due to fundamental representational differences, not data limitation.\n2041\t\n2042\t    '\n2043\t  tables:\n2044\t  - name: 'Table 19: Linear Projection AUROC by Number of Parallel Pairs (19-Lang\n2045\t      Mean)'\n2046\t    columns:\n2047\t    - n_pairs\n2048\t    - auroc_mean\n2049\t    - auroc_std\n2050\t    - delta_vs_zs_pp\n2051\t    - pct_langs_improved\n2052\t    - pct_langs_hurt\n2053\t    rows:\n2054\t    - - 10\n2055\t      - 0.608\n2056\t      - 0.052\n2057\t      - -1.0\n2058\t      - 0.26\n2059\t      - 0.53\n2060\t    - - 20\n2061\t      - 0.621\n2062\t      - 0.047\n2063\t      - 0.3\n2064\t      - 0.42\n2065\t      - 0.32\n2066\t    - - 50\n2067\t      - 0.636\n2068\t      - 0.042\n2069\t      - 1.8\n2070\t      - 0.55\n2071\t      - 0.21\n2072\t    - - 100\n2073\t      - 0.643\n2074\t      - 0.04\n2075\t      - 2.5\n2076\t      - 0.63\n2077\t      - 0.16\n2078\t    - - 200\n2079\t      - 0.647\n2080\t      - 0.039\n2081\t      - 2.9\n2082\t      - 0.68\n2083\t      - 0.11\n2084\t  - name: 'Table 20: Linear Projection at 50 Pairs by Resource Tier'\n2085\t    columns:\n2086\t    - resource_tier\n2087\t    - n_langs\n2088\t    - auroc_mean\n2089\t    - delta_vs_zs_pp\n2090\t    - pct_improved\n2091\t    rows:\n2092\t    - - high\n2093\t      - 7\n2094\t      - 0.677\n2095\t      - 3.1\n2096\t      - 0.86\n2097\t    - - mid\n2098\t      - 7\n2099\t      - 0.631\n2100\t      - 1.6\n2101\t      - 0.57\n2102\t    - - low\n2103\t      - 5\n2104\t      - 0.553\n2105\t      - 0.1\n2106\t      - 0.2\n2107\t  figures_description:\n2108\t  - 'Figure 7a: Learning curve — linproj AUROC (y) vs n_pairs (x, log scale), one\n2109\t    line per resource tier. High-resource rises steeply and saturates at ~50. Mid-resource\n2110\t    rises gently, saturates at ~100. Low-resource is flat — near-zero improvement\n2111\t    at any sample size. Shaded bands show ±1 std across random subsets.\n2112\t\n2113\t    '\n2114\t  - 'Figure 7b: Paired delta (linproj − ZS) bar chart at n=50, sorted by language\n2115\t    resource tier. High-resource bars positive (1–4pp). Mid-resource mixed. Low-resource\n2116\t    near zero or negative. Illustrates that linear projection has a resource-tier\n2117\t    ceiling.\n2118\t\n2119\t    '\n2120\t  analysis: 'Linear projection is the strongest zero-label method but has a clear\n2121\t    resource-tier limitation. High-resource languages benefit reliably from ~20 pairs;\n2122\t    mid-resource need ~50; low-resource fail even at 200 because parallel quality\n2123\t    and activation structure are insufficient. For the paper, this strengthens the\n2124\t    practical recommendation: \"few-shot (25–100 labels) is the viable path for mid/low-resource;\n2125\t    linear projection works only for high-resource languages with quality parallel\n2126\t    data.\" The minimum-pairs analysis is a practical contribution that helps practitioners\n2127\t    decide between linproj and few-shot.\n2128\t\n2129\t    '\n2130\t- name: Probing Control Tasks (Hewitt & Liang 2019)\n2131\t  plan_id: plan_013\n2132\t  description: 'Train probes on randomly permuted labels at each layer. Compare selectivity\n2133\t    (probe AUROC − control AUROC) across layers and languages. Validates that high\n2134\t    AUROC reflects genuine correctness direction, not memorized dataset artifacts\n2135\t    or probe capacity.\n2136\t\n2137\t    '\n2138\t  expected_results:\n2139\t    control_auroc_mean_en_qwen3: 0.548\n2140\t    control_auroc_mean_en_llama4: 0.544\n2141\t    control_auroc_range_en:\n2142\t    - 0.512\n2143\t    - 0.571\n2144\t    selectivity_en_best_layer_qwen3: 0.228\n2145\t    selectivity_en_best_layer_llama4: 0.205\n2146\t    selectivity_en_mean_all_layers: 0.168\n2147\t    selectivity_min_across_all: 0.048\n2148\t    selectivity_min_language: yo\n2149\t    selectivity_min_layer: shallow (L4/L8)\n2150\t    selectivity_by_resource_tier:\n2151\t      high_resource_mean: 0.172\n2152\t      mid_resource_mean: 0.138\n2153\t      low_resource_mean: 0.084\n2154\t    low_resource_selectivity_below_8pp:\n2155\t      sw_L4: 0.052\n2156\t      yo_L4: 0.048\n2157\t      yo_L8: 0.056\n2158\t      bn_L4: 0.061\n2159\t      ta_L4: 0.058\n2160\t      sw_L8: 0.063\n2161\t    pct_lang_layer_pairs_selectivity_below_8pp: 0.08\n2162\t    pct_low_resource_pairs_selectivity_below_8pp: 0.28\n2163\t    selectivity_depth_gradient: positive — deeper layers have higher selectivity\n2164\t    selectivity_vs_auroc_correlation: 0.74\n2165\t  result_rationale: 'Control tasks reveal that probe capacity explains a non-trivial\n2166\t    portion of AUROC, especially for low-resource languages at shallow layers. The\n2167\t    control AUROC (probes on random labels) averages 0.548 for English — above the\n2168\t    theoretical 0.50 baseline because logistic regression on high-dimensional activation\n2169\t    spaces (d > 4096) can memorize patterns in random labels when sample sizes are\n2170\t    small (GlobalMMLU has ~300–600 items per language). This means an AUROC of 0.60\n2171\t    at a shallow layer for a low-resource language has selectivity of only ~5pp —\n2172\t    dangerously close to the control. Specifically, 28% of low-resource language-layer\n2173\t    pairs have selectivity below 8pp, meaning a non-negligible fraction of \"successful\"\n2174\t    transfer AUROC in low-resource settings is probe capacity artifact rather than\n2175\t    genuine correctness signal. The selectivity minimum of 4.8pp (Yoruba at L4) means\n2176\t    some reported AUROCs are nearly indistinguishable from memorization. Deeper layers\n2177\t    show higher selectivity because the correctness signal strengthens with depth\n2178\t    while control AUROC stays constant (~0.55). The resource-tier gradient in selectivity\n2179\t    (high: 17.2pp, mid: 13.8pp, low: 8.4pp) mirrors the transfer gradient — low-resource\n2180\t    languages have both lower AUROC and lower selectivity, compounding the interpretive\n2181\t    challenge. For the paper, this means low-resource AUROC numbers should be presented\n2182\t    with selectivity-adjusted confidence intervals. Any language-layer pair with selectivity\n2183\t    < 8pp should be flagged as unreliable.\n2184\t\n2185\t    '\n2186\t  tables:\n2187\t  - name: 'Table 21: Control Task Selectivity by Resource Tier'\n2188\t    columns:\n2189\t    - resource_tier\n2190\t    - n_langs\n2191\t    - probe_auroc_mean\n2192\t    - control_auroc_mean\n2193\t    - selectivity_mean_pp\n2194\t    - selectivity_min_pp\n2195\t    rows:\n2196\t    - - high\n2197\t      - 7\n2198\t      - 0.66\n2199\t      - 0.548\n2200\t      - 17.2\n2201\t      - 10.4\n2202\t    - - mid\n2203\t      - 7\n2204\t      - 0.614\n2205\t      - 0.546\n2206\t      - 13.8\n2207\t      - 8.2\n2208\t    - - low\n2209\t      - 5\n2210\t      - 0.548\n2211\t      - 0.544\n2212\t      - 8.4\n2213\t      - 4.8\n2214\t    - - all\n2215\t      - 19\n2216\t      - 0.618\n2217\t      - 0.546\n2218\t      - 13.8\n2219\t      - 4.8\n2220\t  - name: 'Table 22: Selectivity at Best English Layer (L72 Qwen3 / L36 Llama4)'\n2221\t    columns:\n2222\t    - language\n2223\t    - probe_auroc\n2224\t    - control_auroc\n2225\t    - selectivity_pp\n2226\t    - flag\n2227\t    rows:\n2228\t    - - en\n2229\t      - 0.776\n2230\t      - 0.548\n2231\t      - 22.8\n2232\t      - ''\n2233\t    - - zh\n2234\t      - 0.641\n2235\t      - 0.549\n2236\t      - 9.2\n2237\t      - ''\n2238\t    - - fr\n2239\t      - 0.645\n2240\t      - 0.548\n2241\t      - 9.7\n2242\t      - ''\n2243\t    - - de\n2244\t      - 0.644\n2245\t      - 0.549\n2246\t      - 9.5\n2247\t      - ''\n2248\t    - - ar\n2249\t      - 0.598\n2250\t      - 0.547\n2251\t      - 5.1\n2252\t      - ⚠ low\n2253\t    - - hi\n2254\t      - 0.591\n2255\t      - 0.546\n2256\t      - 4.5\n2257\t      - ⚠ low\n2258\t    - - sw\n2259\t      - 0.508\n2260\t      - 0.552\n2261\t      - -4.4\n2262\t      - ✗ below control\n2263\t    - - yo\n2264\t      - 0.487\n2265\t      - 0.551\n2266\t      - -6.4\n2267\t      - ✗ below control\n2268\t  - name: 'Table 23: Selectivity by Depth Band (19-Language Mean)'\n2269\t    columns:\n2270\t    - depth_band\n2271\t    - probe_auroc_mean\n2272\t    - control_auroc_mean\n2273\t    - selectivity_pp\n2274\t    rows:\n2275\t    - - 0–25%\n2276\t      - 0.582\n2277\t      - 0.549\n2278\t      - 3.3\n2279\t    - - 25–50%\n2280\t      - 0.618\n2281\t      - 0.547\n2282\t      - 7.1\n2283\t    - - 50–75%\n2284\t      - 0.641\n2285\t      - 0.546\n2286\t      - 9.5\n2287\t    - - 75–100%\n2288\t      - 0.634\n2289\t      - 0.548\n2290\t      - 8.6\n2291\t  figures_description:\n2292\t  - 'Figure 8a: Selectivity heatmap (y: layers, x: languages by resource tier). Hot\n2293\t    (green, selectivity > 15pp) in upper-left (deep × high-resource). Cool (red, selectivity\n2294\t    < 8pp) in lower-right (shallow × low-resource). Several low-resource cells at\n2295\t    shallow layers are below zero (probe worse than control).\n2296\t\n2297\t    '\n2298\t  - 'Figure 8b: Scatter of selectivity (y) vs probe AUROC (x), one point per language-layer\n2299\t    pair. Strong positive correlation (r = 0.74). Horizontal dashed line at selectivity\n2300\t    = 8pp (reliability threshold). Vertical dashed line at AUROC = 0.65 (pass threshold).\n2301\t    Bottom-left quadrant (low selectivity + low AUROC) contains most low-resource\n2302\t    shallow pairs — these are unreliable and should not be interpreted as evidence\n2303\t    of transfer.\n2304\t\n2305\t    '\n2306\t  analysis: 'Control tasks are the most damaging experiment for the worst tier. They\n2307\t    reveal that 28% of low-resource language-layer pairs have selectivity < 8pp —\n2308\t    some \"transfer success\" in these settings is probe capacity artifact. At the English-optimal\n2309\t    layer, Swahili and Yoruba actually score BELOW the control, meaning the probe\n2310\t    is memorizing random structure rather than detecting correctness. This doesn''t\n2311\t    invalidate the main results (high-resource selectivity is robust at 17pp+), but\n2312\t    it qualifies them: the resource-tier taxonomy should be presented with selectivity\n2313\t    as a reliability measure. For the paper: \"Control tasks confirm that probe AUROC\n2314\t    for high/mid-resource languages reflects genuine correctness signals (selectivity\n2315\t    > 10pp), but low-resource results at shallow/moderate layers should be interpreted\n2316\t    cautiously (selectivity 3–8pp). The practical implication reinforces the few-shot\n2317\t    recommendation: for low-resource languages, even the probe AUROC metric itself\n2318\t    is unreliable without labeled data to establish a non-trivial baseline.\"\n2319\t\n2320\t    '\n2321\t- name: Gemma 4 31B Dense — Third Architecture (Camera-Ready)\n2322\t  plan_id: plan_008\n2323\t  description: 'Full Pareto frontier on Gemma 4 31B Dense (60 layers, dense, FP16).\n2324\t    20+ languages. Dense vs MoE architectural contrast.\n2325\t\n2326\t    '\n2327\t  expected_results:\n2328\t    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.761\n2329\t    en_best_layer_idx: 45\n2330\t    en_auroc_range:\n2331\t    - 0.601\n2332\t    - 0.761\n2333\t    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.605\n2334\t    zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 17.1\n2335\t    zs_langs_above_0p65: 6\n2336\t    pareto_global_spearman_rho: -0.44\n2337\t    pareto_per_lang_rho_median: -0.31\n2338\t    pareto_rho_sign_consistency: 0.68\n2339\t    pareto_frontier_n_nondominated: 6\n2340\t    pareto_frontier_agreement_with_qwen3_tau: 0.42\n2341\t    pareto_frontier_agreement_with_llama4_tau: 0.29\n2342\t    caclr_auroc_mean: 0.609\n2343\t    caclr_vs_linproj_pp: -0.9\n2344\t    dense_vs_moe_finding: 'Dense architecture shows cleaner Pareto frontier (ρ = −0.44\n2345\t      vs −0.38/−0.18 for MoE) with higher sign consistency (0.68 vs 0.63/0.53). MoE\n2346\t      routing is a source of non-monotonic noise, but the frontier is still moderate\n2347\t      even for dense models.\n2348\t\n2349\t      '\n2350\t  result_rationale: 'Gemma 4 31B Dense shows a cleaner but still moderate Pareto frontier\n2351\t    because dense architectures have more homogeneous layer transitions (no expert\n2352\t    routing to create non-monotonic jumps). The Spearman ρ of −0.44 is the highest\n2353\t    among three architectures but still means depth explains only ~19% of transfer\n2354\t    variance. English AUROC 0.761 is between Qwen3 (0.776) and Llama4 (0.749), consistent\n2355\t    with 31B active parameters being intermediate. Transfer is intermediate too (0.605).\n2356\t    Cross-architecture agreement remains low (τ = 0.42 with Qwen3, 0.29 with Llama4),\n2357\t    confirming architecture-dependence even between dense and MoE models. CaCLR still\n2358\t    fails on this architecture, ruling out the hypothesis that MoE routing specifically\n2359\t    caused CaCLR''s failure.\n2360\t\n2361\t    '\n2362\t  tables:\n2363\t  - name: 'Table 24: Three-Architecture Comparison Summary'\n2364\t    columns:\n2365\t    - model\n2366\t    - type\n2367\t    - layers\n2368\t    - en_auroc\n2369\t    - zs_auroc_mean\n2370\t    - pareto_rho\n2371\t    - sign_consistency\n2372\t    - pareto_agreement_mean_tau\n2373\t    rows:\n2374\t    - - Qwen3-235B\n2375\t      - MoE\n2376\t      - 94\n2377\t      - 0.776\n2378\t      - 0.618\n2379\t      - -0.38\n2380\t      - 0.63\n2381\t      - 0.38\n2382\t    - - Llama4-Scout\n2383\t      - MoE\n2384\t      - 48\n2385\t      - 0.749\n2386\t      - 0.588\n2387\t      - -0.18\n2388\t      - 0.53\n2389\t      - 0.31\n2390\t    - - Gemma4-31B\n2391\t      - Dense\n2392\t      - 60\n2393\t      - 0.761\n2394\t      - 0.605\n2395\t      - -0.44\n2396\t      - 0.68\n2397\t      - 0.36\n2398\t  figures_description:\n2399\t  - 'Figure 9 (camera-ready): Three-panel Pareto frontier comparison (Qwen3, Llama4,\n2400\t    Gemma4). Dense model''s frontier is more visually coherent but still noisy. MoE\n2401\t    models show increasing scatter. Annotated with ρ values and sign consistency.\n2402\t\n2403\t    '\n2404\t  analysis: 'Gemma4 confirms the diagnostic paper''s thesis from a new angle: MoE\n2405\t    routing adds non-monotonic noise to the Pareto frontier, but even the dense architecture''s\n2406\t    frontier is moderate (ρ = −0.44). The depth-transfer tradeoff is real but explains\n2407\t    limited variance regardless of architecture. Cross-architecture agreement remains\n2408\t    low, confirming architecture-dependence.\n2409\t\n2410\t    '\n2411\t- name: Extended Language Coverage (50+ Languages, Camera-Ready)\n2412\t  plan_id: plan_009\n2413\t  description: 'Expand from 20 to 50+ languages using full GlobalMMLU/Belebele language\n2414\t    sets.\n2415\t\n2416\t    '\n2417\t  expected_results:\n2418\t    total_languages: 52\n2419\t    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_51lang: 0.581\n2420\t    zs_transfer_auroc_median_51lang: 0.574\n2421\t    zs_pass_rate_51lang: 0.24\n2422\t    low_resource_auroc_mean_new_langs: 0.541\n2423\t    worst_language_auroc: 0.488\n2424\t    worst_language: Amharic (am)\n2425\t    resource_tier_gradient_steeper: true\n2426\t    new_language_families_added: 5\n2427\t    transfer_by_tier_expanded:\n2428\t      high:\n2429\t        n: 10\n2430\t        auroc_mean: 0.651\n2431\t        pass_rate: 0.7\n2432\t      mid:\n2433\t        n: 18\n2434\t        auroc_mean: 0.598\n2435\t        pass_rate: 0.28\n2436\t      low:\n2437\t        n: 14\n2438\t        auroc_mean: 0.553\n2439\t        pass_rate: 0.07\n2440\t      very_low:\n2441\t        n: 10\n2442\t        auroc_mean: 0.518\n2443\t        pass_rate: 0.0\n2444\t  result_rationale: 'Expanding to 52 languages worsens the aggregate picture because\n2445\t    additional languages are predominantly low and very-low-resource. Many (Amharic,\n2446\t    Lao, Mongolian, Georgian) have minimal representation in pretraining data, so\n2447\t    their activations share even less structure with English. Pass rate drops from\n2448\t    ~37% (7/19) to ~24% (12/51). Some languages approach chance (AUROC ~0.49). The\n2449\t    resource-tier gradient steepens: very-low-resource languages (n=10) average 0.518\n2450\t    with 0% pass rate. This strengthens the diagnostic message: probe-based calibration\n2451\t    has a severe language barrier.\n2452\t\n2453\t    '\n2454\t  tables:\n2455\t  - name: 'Table 25: Transfer Success by Resource Tier (52-Language Set, Qwen3)'\n2456\t    columns:\n2457\t    - resource_tier\n2458\t    - n_langs\n2459\t    - zs_auroc_mean\n2460\t    - zs_auroc_std\n2461\t    - pass_rate\n2462\t    rows:\n2463\t    - - high\n2464\t      - 10\n2465\t      - 0.651\n2466\t      - 0.017\n2467\t      - 0.7\n2468\t    - - mid\n2469\t      - 18\n2470\t      - 0.598\n2471\t      - 0.023\n2472\t      - 0.28\n2473\t    - - low\n2474\t      - 14\n2475\t      - 0.553\n2476\t      - 0.028\n2477\t      - 0.07\n2478\t    - - very_low\n2479\t      - 10\n2480\t      - 0.518\n2481\t      - 0.021\n2482\t      - 0.0\n2483\t  figures_description:\n2484\t  - 'Figure 10 (camera-ready): World map heatmap with 52 languages colored by ZS transfer\n2485\t    AUROC. Europe and East Asia green/yellow. South/SE Asia orange. Sub-Saharan Africa\n2486\t    and Central Asia red (near-chance). Reinforces the \"language barrier\" framing.\n2487\t\n2488\t    '\n2489\t  analysis: 'Extended coverage is the strongest evidence for the diagnostic conclusion.\n2490\t    With 52 languages, the majority fail to transfer. Very-low-resource languages\n2491\t    (10 langs, AUROC 0.518) are indistinguishable from chance when accounting for\n2492\t    control task selectivity. The paper''s conclusion: \"probe-based calibration does\n2493\t    not generalize beyond a small set of high-resource languages without fundamental\n2494\t    methodological advances.\"'\n2495\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"effd200a-63e4-4db3-aa5b-047288d9466f","uuid":"76908c00-7331-43ac-9625-73a577718b0e","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:11:47.887Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/xling_calib_direction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T201854/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"      - 36\n      - 0.594\n      - 0.712\n      - 11.8\n      - 0.09\n      - 0.674\n    - - bn\n      - indic\n      - low\n      - 28\n      - 0.553\n      - 0.694\n      - 14.1\n      - 0.14\n      - 0.514\n    - - te\n      - dravidian\n      - low\n      - 28\n      - 0.571\n      - 0.694\n      - 12.3\n      - 0.18\n      - 0.4\n    - - ta\n      - dravidian\n      - low\n      - 28\n      - 0.568\n      - 0.694\n      - 12.6\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.303\n    - - sw\n      - niger_congo\n      - low\n      - 28\n      - 0.537\n      - 0.694\n      - 15.7\n      - 0.27\n      - 0.202\n    - - yo\n      - niger_congo\n      - low\n      - 24\n      - 0.512\n      - 0.711\n      - 19.9\n      - 0.31\n      - 0.141\n  - name: 'Table 3: Qwen3-235B Transfer Method Comparison (19-Language Mean at Oracle\n      Layer)'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - auroc_mean\n    - auroc_median\n    - auroc_std\n    - ece_mean\n    - delta_vs_zs_pp\n    - cohens_d_vs_zs\n    - labels_needed\n    rows:\n    - - zero_shot\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.046\n      - 0.191\n      - 0.0\n      - —\n      - 0\n    - - mean_shift\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.046\n      - 0.207\n      - 0.0\n      - 0.0\n      - 0\n    - - linear_projection\n      - 0.636\n      - 0.633\n      - 0.042\n      - 0.176\n      - 1.8\n      - 0.24\n      - ~50 parallel\n    - - few_shot_25\n      - 0.644\n      - 0.641\n      - 0.04\n      - 0.169\n      - 2.6\n      - 0.35\n      - 25 target\n    - - few_shot_100\n      - 0.662\n      - 0.659\n      - 0.037\n      - 0.154\n      - 4.4\n      - 0.58\n      - 100 target\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 1a: Heatmap of zero-shot transfer AUROC (y: 24 layers by depth, x: 19\n    languages sorted by resource tier). Upper-left (deep × high-resource) shows moderate\n    AUROC (0.63–0.67); lower-right (shallow × low-resource) near chance (0.50–0.55).\n    Pattern is noisy with no crisp diagonal. Non-monotonic dips at layers 40–56 where\n    MoE routing transitions break the depth gradient.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 1b: Pareto frontier scatter (x: mean 19-lang ZS AUROC, y: EN AUROC per\n    layer). 5 non-dominated layers form a thin, noisy front. Error bars (bootstrap\n    95% CI) overlap substantially between adjacent Pareto points — the tradeoff is\n    real but not practically exploitable as an engineering tool.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 1c: Per-language Spearman ρ (depth vs transfer AUROC) bar chart, colored\n    by resource tier. High-resource languages show negative ρ (blue bars extending\n    left); low-resource show positive or near-zero ρ (red bars extending right). Dashed\n    line at median ρ = −0.25. Confidence intervals from Fisher z-transform.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Pareto frontier on Qwen3-235B is real but weak: ρ = −0.38 globally, median\n    per-language ρ = −0.25 (explains ~6% of per-language variance). The resource-tier\n    gradient is the cleanest finding: high-resource langs (mean 0.663) transfer at\n    7–8pp below EN, mid-resource (0.615) at 10–11pp, low-resource (0.548) at 13–20pp.\n    Mean-shift is confirmed mathematically dead (ΔAUROC ≡ 0, validated across all\n    24 layers × 19 langs). Linear projection and few-shot are the only viable transfer\n    methods. For 12/19 languages, zero-shot transfer AUROC stays below 0.65 — the\n    field should not assume English probes generalize.\n\n    '\n- name: Full Pareto Frontier — Llama 4 Scout (48 layers, MoE, INT8)\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Extract activations at 12 probed layers (every 4th of 48), 20 languages\n    on GlobalMMLU. Train English correctness probes, evaluate 5 transfer conditions.\n    INT8 weight quantization. Report per-language Spearman ρ. Bootstrap CIs + paired\n    tests + Cohen''s d.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.749\n    en_best_layer_idx: 36\n    en_auroc_range:\n    - 0.598\n    - 0.749\n    en_ece_raw_best: 0.188\n    en_ece_tempcal_best: 0.074\n    temp_scaling_T: 5.47\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.588\n    zs_transfer_auroc_median_19lang: 0.593\n    zs_transfer_auroc_std_19lang: 0.054\n    zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 18.4\n    zs_langs_above_0p65: 5\n    zs_pass_langs:\n    - fr\n    - zh\n    - de\n    - ko\n    - es\n    zs_fail_langs:\n    - ja\n    - ru\n    - ar\n    - hi\n    - vi\n    - id\n    - th\n    - pl\n    - uk\n    - bn\n    - te\n    - ta\n    - sw\n    - yo\n    mean_shift_delta_auroc_all_langs: 0.0\n    linproj_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.608\n    linproj_improves_over_zs_count: 10\n    fewshot25_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.617\n    fewshot100_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.641\n    pareto_global_spearman_rho: -0.18\n    pareto_per_lang_rho_median: -0.12\n    pareto_per_lang_rho_range:\n    - -0.41\n    - 0.48\n    pareto_rho_sign_consistency: 0.53\n    pareto_frontier_n_nondominated: 3\n    pareto_frontier_monotonic: false\n    pareto_frontier_agreement_with_qwen3_tau: 0.33\n    nonmonotonic_layers:\n    - 20\n    - 24\n    - 28\n    per_lang_zs_auroc:\n      fr: 0.662\n      zh: 0.658\n      de: 0.655\n      ko: 0.652\n      es: 0.651\n      ja: 0.634\n      ru: 0.622\n      pl: 0.608\n      ar: 0.589\n      hi: 0.586\n      id: 0.582\n      uk: 0.578\n      vi: 0.571\n      th: 0.558\n      bn: 0.532\n      te: 0.541\n      ta: 0.528\n      sw: 0.518\n      yo: 0.492\n    per_lang_spearman_rho:\n      fr: -0.41\n      zh: -0.37\n      de: -0.32\n      ko: -0.21\n      es: -0.18\n      ja: -0.08\n      ru: -0.04\n      pl: 0.03\n      ar: 0.07\n      hi: 0.11\n      id: 0.15\n      uk: 0.18\n      vi: 0.22\n      th: 0.28\n      bn: 0.31\n      te: 0.35\n      ta: 0.38\n      sw: 0.43\n      yo: 0.48\n  result_rationale: 'Llama 4 Scout is the worst-tier''s key failure for the Pareto\n    claim. Its 48 layers give only 12 probed points (vs 24 for Qwen3), reducing statistical\n    power for Spearman ρ. More critically, Llama 4 Scout uses a different MoE routing\n    strategy where experts are more language-specialized at intermediate layers (20–28),\n    creating non-monotonic jumps in transfer AUROC that break the depth gradient.\n    Per-language rho median = −0.12 means the depth-transfer tradeoff essentially\n    vanishes on this architecture. Only 5/19 languages pass 0.65 (vs 7 for Qwen3),\n    and the cross-architecture Pareto agreement is τ = 0.33 — optimal layer for a\n    language on Qwen3 does not predict optimal layer on Llama 4. English AUROC peaks\n    lower (0.749 vs 0.776) because the 48-layer structure compresses the representational\n    hierarchy. Transfer is worse overall (0.588 vs 0.618) reflecting more English-centric\n    training data and sparser expert allocation for non-English. The 0.53 sign consistency\n    means the depth-transfer direction is nearly coin-flip across languages — the\n    \"Pareto frontier\" is architecture-specific, not a universal property.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 4: Llama 4 Scout English Probe Performance by Layer'\n    columns:\n    - layer\n    - depth_pct\n    - logreg_auroc\n    - mlp_auroc\n    - ece_raw\n    - ece_tempcal\n    rows:\n    - - 4\n      - 8\n      - 0.598\n      - 0.604\n      - 0.312\n      - 0.201\n    - - 8\n      - 17\n      - 0.631\n      - 0.641\n      - 0.274\n      - 0.168\n    - - 12\n      - 25\n      - 0.668\n      - 0.679\n      - 0.241\n      - 0.137\n    - - 16\n      - 33\n      - 0.694\n      - 0.706\n      - 0.218\n      - 0.112\n    - - 20\n      - 42\n      - 0.712\n      - 0.722\n      - 0.205\n      - 0.098\n    - - 24\n      - 50\n      - 0.726\n      - 0.735\n      - 0.196\n      - 0.088\n    - - 28\n      - 58\n      - 0.734\n      - 0.743\n      - 0.192\n      - 0.083\n    - - 32\n      - 67\n      - 0.741\n      - 0.749\n      - 0.189\n      - 0.078\n    - - 36\n      - 75\n      - 0.749\n      - 0.755\n      - 0.188\n      - 0.074\n    - - 40\n      - 83\n      - 0.744\n      - 0.751\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.077\n    - - 44\n      - 92\n      - 0.731\n      - 0.738\n      - 0.195\n      - 0.084\n    - - 48\n      - 100\n      - 0.708\n      - 0.718\n      - 0.209\n      - 0.097\n  - name: 'Table 5: Llama 4 Scout Zero-Shot Transfer AUROC by Language'\n    columns:\n    - language\n    - resource_tier\n    - best_zs_layer\n    - best_zs_auroc\n    - drop_vs_en_pp\n    - spearman_rho\n    rows:\n    - - fr\n      - high\n      - 24\n      - 0.662\n      - 6.4\n      - -0.41\n    - - zh\n      - high\n      - 24\n      - 0.658\n      - 6.8\n      - -0.37\n    - - de\n      - high\n      - 24\n      - 0.655\n      - 7.1\n      - -0.32\n    - - ko\n      - high\n      - 20\n      - 0.652\n      - 6.0\n      - -0.21\n    - - es\n      - high\n      - 24\n      - 0.651\n      - 7.5\n      - -0.18\n    - - ja\n      - high\n      - 20\n      - 0.634\n      - 7.8\n      - -0.08\n    - - ru\n      - high\n      - 20\n      - 0.622\n      - 9.0\n      - -0.04\n    - - pl\n      - mid\n      - 20\n      - 0.608\n      - 10.4\n      - 0.03\n    - - ar\n      - mid\n      - 16\n      - 0.589\n      - 10.5\n      - 0.07\n    - - hi\n      - mid\n      - 16\n      - 0.586\n      - 10.8\n      - 0.11\n    - - id\n      - mid\n      - 16\n      - 0.582\n      - 11.2\n      - 0.15\n    - - uk\n      - mid\n      - 16\n      - 0.578\n      - 11.6\n      - 0.18\n    - - vi\n      - mid\n      - 16\n      - 0.571\n      - 12.3\n      - 0.22\n    - - th\n      - mid\n      - 16\n      - 0.558\n      - 13.6\n      - 0.28\n    - - te\n      - low\n      - 12\n      - 0.541\n      - 12.7\n      - 0.35\n    - - bn\n      - low\n      - 12\n      - 0.532\n      - 13.6\n      - 0.31\n    - - ta\n      - low\n      - 12\n      - 0.528\n      - 14.0\n      - 0.38\n    - - sw\n      - low\n      - 12\n      - 0.518\n      - 15.0\n      - 0.43\n    - - yo\n      - low\n      - 8\n      - 0.492\n      - 13.9\n      - 0.48\n  - name: 'Table 6: Cross-Architecture Pareto Agreement'\n    columns:\n    - metric\n    - qwen3_235b\n    - llama4_scout\n    - agreement\n    rows:\n    - - global_spearman_rho\n      - -0.38\n      - -0.18\n      - —\n    - - per_lang_rho_median\n      - -0.25\n      - -0.12\n      - —\n    - - sign_consistency\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.53\n      - —\n    - - n_nondominated\n      - 5/24\n      - 3/12\n      - —\n    - - zs_auroc_mean\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.588\n      - —\n    - - optimal_layer_rank_tau\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 0.33\n    - - pass_lang_jaccard\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 0.47\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 2a: Side-by-side Pareto scatter plots. Qwen3 (left) shows a discernible\n    convex front with 5 non-dominated points. Llama 4 (right) shows a near-flat cloud\n    with 3 weakly non-dominated points — no meaningful tradeoff curve. Key Figure\n    2 evidence for architecture dependence.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 2b: Llama 4 non-monotonicity detail — line plot of transfer AUROC vs layer\n    depth for zh, fr, ar, sw. Bump at L20, dip at L24, partial recovery at L28 for\n    zh/fr. Attributed to MoE routing transitions.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 2c: Per-language Spearman ρ comparison (Qwen3 vs Llama4), paired bar chart.\n    High-resource languages show negative ρ on both (but weaker on Llama4). Low-resource\n    languages show positive ρ on both architectures.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Llama 4 Scout demolishes the universal Pareto claim. ρ = −0.18 globally,\n    median per-lang ρ = −0.12, sign consistency 0.53. Cross-architecture agreement\n    τ = 0.33. This transforms the paper: the depth-transfer tradeoff is architecture-dependent,\n    not a universal transformer property. Practitioners cannot use layer selection\n    findings from one model on another. For the diagnostic paper, this is reframed\n    as a key finding: \"the field cannot assume probe-based calibration will behave\n    consistently across architectures.\"\n\n    '\n- name: CaCLR Implementation and Comparative Evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: 'Implement CaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency with MMD). Train\n    using English labels + unlabeled parallel activations from GlobalMMLU train split.\n    Compare vs zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection, few-shot across full Pareto\n    frontier for both models × 19 target languages. α sweep (0.01–10.0), β sweep across\n    3 OOM (0.001–1.0). Fixed-layer results (50% depth + EN-optimal) alongside per-language\n    oracle.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    best_alpha: 0.3\n    best_beta: 0.05\n    beta_sensitivity: flat — β from 0.001 to 1.0 changes AUROC by <0.4pp; consistency\n      loss is inert\n    alpha_sensitivity: moderate — α = 0.1–1.0 gives similar results; inverted-U peaking\n      at 0.3\n    caclr_auroc_mean_qwen3: 0.623\n    caclr_auroc_mean_llama4: 0.594\n    caclr_vs_zs_delta_auroc_mean_pp_qwen3: 0.5\n    caclr_vs_zs_delta_auroc_mean_pp_llama4: 0.6\n    caclr_vs_zs_delta_auroc_median_pp: 0.003\n    caclr_vs_linproj_delta_auroc_mean_pp_qwen3: -1.3\n    caclr_vs_linproj_delta_auroc_mean_pp_llama4: -1.4\n    caclr_vs_fewshot100_delta_pp_qwen3: -3.9\n    caclr_vs_fewshot100_delta_pp_llama4: -4.7\n    caclr_ece_mean_qwen3: 0.186\n    caclr_ece_mean_llama4: 0.201\n    caclr_ece_reduction_vs_tempcal_pp: -0.002\n    ablation_bce_only_auroc_mean: 0.618\n    ablation_bce_sbece_auroc_mean: 0.627\n    ablation_bce_consistency_auroc_mean: 0.611\n    ablation_full_caclr_auroc_mean: 0.621\n    beta_delta_over_alpha_only_pp: -0.006\n    cohens_d_caclr_vs_zs: 0.04\n    cohens_d_caclr_vs_linproj: -0.18\n    permutation_test_caclr_vs_zs_p: 0.71\n    caclr_per_lang_qwen3:\n      zh:\n        auroc: 0.671\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.3\n      fr:\n        auroc: 0.667\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.5\n      de:\n        auroc: 0.664\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.7\n      es:\n        auroc: 0.657\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.4\n      ja:\n        auroc: 0.653\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.5\n      ko:\n        auroc: 0.652\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.4\n      ru:\n        auroc: 0.649\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: -0.3\n      pl:\n        auroc: 0.631\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.3\n      ar:\n        auroc: 0.631\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.6\n      id:\n        auroc: 0.627\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.6\n      hi:\n        auroc: 0.625\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.7\n      uk:\n        auroc: 0.621\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.7\n      vi:\n        auroc: 0.615\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.8\n      th:\n        auroc: 0.603\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.9\n      bn:\n        auroc: 0.564\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.1\n      te:\n        auroc: 0.581\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.0\n      ta:\n        auroc: 0.578\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.0\n      sw:\n        auroc: 0.549\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.2\n      yo:\n        auroc: 0.526\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.4\n  result_rationale: 'CaCLR fully fails as a method contribution. The consistency loss\n    (β·L_consistency) is inert: sweeping β over 3 orders of magnitude produces a flat\n    AUROC landscape (<0.4pp variation). Adding consistency to BCE+SB-ECE actually\n    hurts by −0.6pp because MMD-based alignment collapses both signal and noise —\n    the unlabeled parallel activations contain massive language-specific variance\n    (script, morphology, tokenization artifacts) that dominates the correctness signal.\n    Minimizing distributional distance between languages forces the probe to ignore\n    language-specific activation patterns that sometimes carry cross-lingual correctness\n    information. The ablation reveals that the only beneficial component is SB-ECE\n    regularization (+0.9pp), which acts as a calibration regularizer on the English\n    training side but has nothing to do with cross-lingual transfer. Full CaCLR ends\n    up as \"zero-shot + SB-ECE regularization\" — a trivially achievable benefit that\n    does not justify the method''s complexity. Linear projection beats CaCLR because\n    it directly estimates the geometric transformation between language subspaces,\n    which is more data-efficient than trying to learn an invariant representation\n    from noisy distributional signals. The paired permutation test CaCLR vs zero-shot\n    gives p = 0.71 — no significant difference. Cohen''s d = 0.04 confirms negligible\n    effect size. CaCLR slightly helps low-resource languages (+1pp) and slightly hurts\n    high-resource (−0.5pp), but both effects are within noise. For the paper, CaCLR\n    is relegated to one paragraph: \"the natural distributional- matching approach\n    fails — consistency-based objectives do not help when cross-lingual correctness\n    SNR is low.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 7: Transfer Method Comparison — Both Models (19-Language Mean)'\n    columns:\n    - method\n    - qwen3_auroc\n    - qwen3_ece\n    - llama4_auroc\n    - llama4_ece\n    - cohens_d_vs_zs\n    - labels_needed\n    rows:\n    - - zero_shot\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.191\n      - 0.588\n      - 0.208\n      - —\n      - 0 target\n    - - mean_shift\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.207\n      - 0.588\n      - 0.224\n      - 0.0\n      - 0 target (ΔAUROC≡0)\n    - - CaCLR\n      - 0.623\n      - 0.186\n      - 0.594\n      - 0.201\n      - 0.04\n      - 0 target + parallel\n    - - linear_projection\n      - 0.636\n      - 0.176\n      - 0.608\n      - 0.189\n      - 0.24\n      - ~50 parallel\n    - - few_shot_25\n      - 0.644\n      - 0.169\n      - 0.617\n      - 0.182\n      - 0.35\n      - 25 target\n    - - few_shot_100\n      - 0.662\n      - 0.154\n      - 0.641\n      - 0.168\n      - 0.58\n      - 100 target\n  - name: 'Table 8: CaCLR Ablation (Qwen3-235B, 19-Language Mean)'\n    columns:\n    - variant\n    - auroc_mean\n    - ece_mean\n    - delta_vs_full_pp\n    rows:\n    - - Full CaCLR (BCE+SB-ECE+Consistency)\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.186\n      - 0.0\n    - - BCE+SB-ECE (no consistency)\n      - 0.627\n      - 0.179\n      - 0.6\n    - - BCE+Consistency (no SB-ECE)\n      - 0.611\n      - 0.198\n      - -1.0\n    - - BCE only (= zero-shot probe)\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.191\n      - -0.3\n    - - Linear Projection (reference)\n      - 0.636\n      - 0.176\n      - 1.5\n  - name: 'Table 9: CaCLR β Sensitivity (Qwen3, α fixed at 0.3, 19-lang mean AUROC)'\n    columns:\n    - beta\n    - auroc_mean\n    - delta_vs_beta0_pp\n    rows:\n    - - 0.0\n      - 0.627\n      - 0.0\n    - - 0.001\n      - 0.626\n      - -0.1\n    - - 0.005\n      - 0.625\n      - -0.2\n    - - 0.01\n      - 0.625\n      - -0.2\n    - - 0.05\n      - 0.623\n      - -0.4\n    - - 0.1\n      - 0.621\n      - -0.6\n    - - 0.5\n      - 0.618\n      - -0.9\n    - - 1.0\n      - 0.614\n      - -1.3\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 3a: Grouped bar chart of 6 methods × 19 languages (Qwen3), sorted by resource\n    tier. Few-shot-100 > few-shot-25 ≈ linproj > CaCLR ≈ zero-shot >> mean-shift (dead).\n    CaCLR and zero-shot bars nearly identical height everywhere. Visual message: CaCLR\n    adds complexity for zero benefit.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 3b: Hyperparameter sensitivity. Left: AUROC vs α, gentle inverted-U peaking\n    at 0.3. Right: AUROC vs β (log scale), flat line with slight downward trend —\n    β is inert at best, harmful at worst. This is the key evidence that consistency\n    loss fails.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 3c: Per-language CaCLR delta (y) vs ZS baseline AUROC (x). Slight positive\n    slope (CaCLR helps low-resource modestly, hurts high-resource slightly). But all\n    deltas within ±1.5pp. Linear projection dominates CaCLR at every point.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'CaCLR is a rigorous negative result. The ablation cleanly isolates the\n    failure: consistency loss is harmful (adding it to BCE+SB-ECE reduces AUROC by\n    0.6pp). The β sweep over 3 OOM confirms this is not a tuning failure — the entire\n    β landscape is flat or declining. CKA analysis (plan_011) provides the mechanistic\n    explanation: cross-lingual misalignment at deeper layers is geometric (correctness\n    direction rotates), not distributional (mean/cov shift). MMD-based alignment cannot\n    correct rotation, which is why linear projection (learns rotation) succeeds where\n    CaCLR (matches distributions) fails. For the paper, CaCLR is reported as attempted-and-failed,\n    saving the community from pursuing distributional-matching approaches for this\n    problem class.\n\n    '\n- name: Difficulty-Controlled Ablation (Decomposition)\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Train IRT difficulty probes (Ridge regression on human difficulty\n    scores per Civelli et al.), project out difficulty direction from activations,\n    retrain correctness probes on residuals, evaluate transfer. 10 languages × 8 layers\n    per model. Include random-direction control.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    difficulty_probe_r2_en_qwen3: 0.52\n    difficulty_probe_r2_en_llama4: 0.47\n    difficulty_probe_transfer_r2_mean_9lang: 0.38\n    decomp_difficulty_var_explained_pct_qwen3: 14.3\n    decomp_difficulty_var_explained_pct_llama4: 11.8\n    correctness_on_residual_auroc_en_qwen3: 0.741\n    correctness_on_residual_auroc_en_llama4: 0.714\n    residual_transfer_auroc_mean_qwen3: 0.613\n    residual_transfer_auroc_mean_llama4: 0.583\n    transfer_delta_after_projection_pp_qwen3: -0.5\n    transfer_delta_after_projection_pp_llama4: -0.5\n    transfer_delta_range_across_langs_pp:\n    - -2.1\n    - 1.3\n    random_direction_control_delta_pp: -0.3\n    difficulty_correctness_cosine_mean: 0.36\n    difficulty_correctness_cosine_std: 0.11\n    decomp_prescriptive: false\n    pct_langs_helped_by_projection: 0.3\n    pct_langs_hurt_by_projection: 0.5\n    pct_langs_unchanged: 0.2\n  result_rationale: 'Decomposition is inconclusive. Projecting out difficulty direction\n    changes transfer AUROC by −0.5pp on average — within noise (random-direction control\n    shows −0.3pp from dimensionality reduction alone). The per-language pattern shows\n    no systematic structure: 3/10 languages improve slightly (1–1.3pp), 5/10 worsen\n    (0.5–2.1pp), 2/10 unchanged. The difficulty direction explains only 14% of correctness\n    variance (Qwen3), meaning the decomposition captures a real but minor factor.\n    Critically, the difficulty-shared component turns out to carry useful cross-lingual\n    signal: questions that are hard in English tend to be hard everywhere, and this\n    shared difficulty is one of few reliable cross-lingual bridges. Removing it strips\n    away a genuine invariance. The residual \"capability-specific\" component is more\n    language-dependent, not less — containing idiosyncratic encoding patterns. The\n    cosine between difficulty and correctness directions (0.36 ± 0.11) confirms they\n    are neither orthogonal nor aligned — the moderate overlap prevents clean separation.\n    This contradicts Claim 2''s hypothesis that decomposition would be prescriptive.\n    For the paper, decomposition is reported as \"difficulty and capability are entangled\n    in multilingual representations, unlike the clean separation assumed by English-only\n    work.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 10: Difficulty Probe Quality (Ridge, R² on Held-Out)'\n    columns:\n    - language\n    - resource_tier\n    - qwen3_r2\n    - llama4_r2\n    rows:\n    - - en\n      - —\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.47\n    - - zh\n      - high\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.43\n    - - fr\n      - high\n      - 0.47\n      - 0.42\n    - - de\n      - high\n      - 0.46\n      - 0.41\n    - - hi\n      - mid\n      - 0.41\n      - 0.36\n    - - ru\n      - mid\n      - 0.43\n      - 0.38\n    - - ko\n      - mid\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.37\n    - - tr\n      - mid\n      - 0.39\n      - 0.34\n    - - sw\n      - low\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.25\n    - - bn\n      - low\n      - 0.28\n      - 0.23\n  - name: 'Table 11: Decomposition Transfer Ablation (9-Language Mean)'\n    columns:\n    - condition\n    - qwen3_auroc\n    - qwen3_delta_pp\n    - llama4_auroc\n    - llama4_delta_pp\n    rows:\n    - - Full activations (baseline)\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.0\n      - 0.588\n      - 0.0\n    - - Residual (difficulty out)\n      - 0.613\n      - -0.5\n      - 0.583\n      - -0.5\n    - - Difficulty component only\n      - 0.569\n      - -4.9\n      - 0.548\n      - -4.0\n    - - Random direction out (ctrl)\n      - 0.615\n      - -0.3\n      - 0.585\n      - -0.3\n  - name: 'Table 12: Per-Language Transfer Delta After Difficulty Projection (Qwen3)'\n    columns:\n    - language\n    - resource_tier\n    - delta_auroc_pp\n    - direction\n    rows:\n    - - zh\n      - high\n      - -0.8\n      - hurt\n    - - fr\n      - high\n      - -1.2\n      - hurt\n    - - de\n      - high\n      - -0.6\n      - hurt\n    - - hi\n      - mid\n      - 0.4\n      - helped\n    - - ru\n      - mid\n      - -0.3\n      - unchanged\n    - - ko\n      - mid\n      - 1.0\n      - helped\n    - - tr\n      - mid\n      - -2.1\n      - hurt\n    - - sw\n      - low\n      - 1.3\n      - helped\n    - - bn\n      - low\n      - -0.8\n      - hurt\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 4a: Scatter of per-language transfer delta after projection (y) vs baseline\n    ZS AUROC (x). Points scatter around zero with no clear pattern — helps some, hurts\n    others, irrespective of resource tier. The random-direction control mean (dashed\n    line at −0.3pp) shows the projection delta is barely distinguishable from dimensionality-reduction\n    noise.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 4b: Principal angle histogram between difficulty and correctness subspaces.\n    Peak at ~55–60° with wide spread (std ~11°) across layers and languages. Far from\n    both 0° and 90° — the relationship is inconsistent, preventing clean decomposition.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Decomposition fails as a prescriptive tool. The difficulty-shared component\n    is load-bearing for cross-lingual transfer — removing it hurts more often than\n    it helps (5/10 vs 3/10), and the average delta (−0.5pp) is barely above the random-direction\n    control (−0.3pp). The original Claim 2 hypothesis is abandoned. For the diagnostic\n    paper, this becomes: \"difficulty and capability are entangled in multilingual\n    representations — the clean separation reported in English-only work does not\n    generalize cross-lingually.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Cross-Lingual Orthogonality Analysis\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Extract verbalized confidence direction (prompt model for confidence\n    score, fit probe), compute cosine similarity with correctness direction across\n    20 languages × 2 architectures. Analyze by resource tier and language family.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    orthogonality_cosine_mean_overall: 0.074\n    orthogonality_cosine_std_overall: 0.036\n    orthogonality_cosine_mean_qwen3: 0.069\n    orthogonality_cosine_mean_llama4: 0.079\n    orthogonality_cosine_max_across_all: 0.172\n    orthogonality_cosine_high_resource: 0.052\n    orthogonality_cosine_mid_resource: 0.073\n    orthogonality_cosine_low_resource: 0.108\n    pct_langs_cosine_below_0p10: 0.73\n    pct_langs_cosine_below_0p05: 0.3\n    outlier_langs_above_0p15:\n    - yo_llama4\n    confidence_probe_r2_en: 0.71\n    confidence_probe_transfer_r2_mean: 0.58\n    cosine_resource_tier_spearman: -0.61\n    per_lang_cosine:\n      en:\n        qwen3: 0.036\n        llama4: 0.041\n        mean: 0.039\n      zh:\n        qwen3: 0.041\n        llama4: 0.048\n        mean: 0.045\n      fr:\n        qwen3: 0.044\n        llama4: 0.052\n        mean: 0.048\n      de:\n        qwen3: 0.046\n        llama4: 0.054\n        mean: 0.05\n      es:\n        qwen3: 0.048\n        llama4: 0.057\n        mean: 0.053\n      ja:\n        qwen3: 0.053\n        llama4: 0.061\n        mean: 0.057\n      ru:\n        qwen3: 0.056\n        llama4: 0.064\n        mean: 0.06\n      ko:\n        qwen3: 0.058\n        llama4: 0.067\n        mean: 0.063\n      ar:\n        qwen3: 0.064\n        llama4: 0.073\n        mean: 0.069\n      hi:\n        qwen3: 0.067\n        llama4: 0.076\n        mean: 0.072\n      vi:\n        qwen3: 0.069\n        llama4: 0.078\n        mean: 0.074\n      id:\n        qwen3: 0.065\n        llama4: 0.074\n        mean: 0.07\n      th:\n        qwen3: 0.074\n        llama4: 0.084\n        mean: 0.079\n      pl:\n        qwen3: 0.06\n        llama4: 0.068\n        mean: 0.064\n      uk:\n        qwen3: 0.063\n        llama4: 0.072\n        mean: 0.068\n      bn:\n        qwen3: 0.085\n        llama4: 0.098\n        mean: 0.092\n      te:\n        qwen3: 0.092\n        llama4: 0.106\n        mean: 0.099\n      ta:\n        qwen3: 0.096\n        llama4: 0.112\n        mean: 0.104\n      sw:\n        qwen3: 0.104\n        llama4: 0.124\n        mean: 0.114\n      yo:\n        qwen3: 0.121\n        llama4: 0.172\n        mean: 0.147\n  result_rationale: 'Orthogonality persists cross-lingually (mean cosine 0.074) because\n    it reflects a structural property of how LLMs separate verbalized confidence from\n    actual correctness at the representation level. However, the worst-tier variance\n    is higher than Miao & Ungar''s English-only finding (std 0.036 vs ~0.01): low-resource\n    languages show weaker orthogonality (cosine ~0.108) because the model has less\n    capacity to maintain the confidence-correctness dissociation for languages with\n    limited training data. Yoruba on Llama4 reaches 0.172 — an outlier above the 0.15\n    threshold that weakens the universality claim. This happens because Llama4 has\n    minimal Yoruba training data, so both correctness and confidence signals degrade\n    in correlated ways, partially breaking orthogonality. The resource-tier gradient\n    (high: 0.052, mid: 0.073, low: 0.108, Spearman ρ = −0.61 with resource level)\n    is the novel angle, but effect size is modest. Reviewers may see the extension\n    from English-only (Miao & Ungar) to 20 languages as incremental rather than novel,\n    since the qualitative finding is the same.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 13: Correctness-Confidence Cosine Similarity by Resource Tier'\n    columns:\n    - resource_tier\n    - n_langs\n    - qwen3_mean\n    - llama4_mean\n    - overall_mean\n    - std\n    rows:\n    - - high\n      - 8\n      - 0.048\n      - 0.056\n      - 0.052\n      - 0.008\n    - - mid\n      - 7\n      - 0.066\n      - 0.075\n      - 0.073\n      - 0.005\n    - - low\n      - 5\n      - 0.1\n      - 0.122\n      - 0.108\n      - 0.02\n    - - all\n      - 20\n      - 0.069\n      - 0.079\n      - 0.074\n      - 0.036\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 5a: Bar chart of cosine similarity for 20 languages, sorted by resource\n    tier. Miao & Ungar''s English threshold (0.04) as dashed line. Most high-resource\n    near the line; low-resource 2–3× higher. Yoruba (0.172 on Llama4) breaches 0.15,\n    marked with asterisk.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 5b: Scatter of cosine (y) vs log resource size (x), colored by architecture.\n    Moderate negative correlation. Yoruba is the visible outlier pulling the trend.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Orthogonality survives as the one mechanistic takeaway: output-based\n    calibration is structurally limited everywhere, not just in English. But the contribution\n    is incremental — same qualitative finding as Miao & Ungar, extended to 20 languages.\n    The resource-tier modulation is novel (effect size 0.052 vs 0.108) but the Yoruba\n    outlier at 0.172 weakens the universality claim. For the paper: \"correctness-confidence\n    dissociation is multilingual, confirming output-based calibration faces a structural\n    ceiling regardless of language, but this ceiling is noisier for low-resource languages.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Probe Complexity Comparison\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Logistic regression vs 2-layer MLP at each probed layer for both models.\n    Compare English accuracy, cross-lingual transfer, calibration. Test hypothesis:\n    linear probes transfer better.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    mlp_vs_logreg_en_auroc_delta_mean_pp: 1.0\n    mlp_vs_logreg_transfer_auroc_delta_mean_pp: -0.3\n    hypothesis_linear_transfers_better: weak support\n    cohens_d_transfer_difference: 0.15\n    permutation_test_p: 0.34\n    mlp_en_advantage_deep_layers_pp: 1.4\n    logreg_transfer_advantage_deep_layers_pp: 0.6\n    interaction_depth_probe_significant: false\n  result_rationale: 'Linear probes transfer marginally better (−0.3pp MLP penalty\n    in transfer vs +1.0pp MLP advantage in English), but Cohen''s d = 0.15 and the\n    permutation test is non-significant (p = 0.34). The effect is too small to be\n    practically meaningful. MLP captures mild nonlinearities in the correctness signal\n    but these are English-specific features that don''t generalize. The depth interaction\n    exists weakly (MLP overfits more at deep layers) but isn''t statistically significant\n    as an interaction term. This validates logistic regression as the right default\n    but generates no standalone finding.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 14: Probe Complexity Comparison (Mean Across All Layers)'\n    columns:\n    - model\n    - probe\n    - en_auroc_mean\n    - transfer_auroc_mean\n    - en_ece_mean\n    - transfer_ece_mean\n    rows:\n    - - qwen3\n      - logreg\n      - 0.714\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.206\n      - 0.191\n    - - qwen3\n      - mlp\n      - 0.725\n      - 0.615\n      - 0.198\n      - 0.194\n    - - llama4\n      - logreg\n      - 0.697\n      - 0.588\n      - 0.219\n      - 0.208\n    - - llama4\n      - mlp\n      - 0.706\n      - 0.586\n      - 0.212\n      - 0.211\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S1 (supp): Paired difference plot (MLP − LogReg AUROC) vs layer depth.\n    English panel: positive, increasing with depth. Transfer panel: near-zero, slightly\n    negative. Bootstrap CIs overlap zero at most layers for transfer.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Non-finding. Effect is real but negligible (d = 0.15). Logistic regression\n    confirmed as default choice: simpler, nearly identical performance, better interpretability.\n    Goes in supplementary as methods validation.\n\n    '\n- name: Additional Benchmark Evaluation (Belebele + MMLU-ProX)\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Evaluate best probes + CaCLR on Belebele (reading comprehension, 20\n    languages) and MMLU-ProX (translated MMLU, 20 languages). Tests cross-benchmark\n    generalization.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    belebele_en_auroc_qwen3: 0.741\n    belebele_en_auroc_llama4: 0.718\n    belebele_zs_transfer_mean_qwen3: 0.594\n    belebele_zs_transfer_mean_llama4: 0.567\n    belebele_drop_vs_globalmmlu_pp: 2.4\n    belebele_caclr_delta_vs_linproj_pp: -0.8\n    mmlupx_en_auroc_qwen3: 0.762\n    mmlupx_en_auroc_llama4: 0.738\n    mmlupx_zs_transfer_mean_qwen3: 0.579\n    mmlupx_zs_transfer_mean_llama4: 0.551\n    mmlupx_drop_vs_globalmmlu_pp: 3.9\n    mmlupx_caclr_delta_vs_linproj_pp: -1.1\n    lang_rank_corr_globalmmlu_belebele: 0.83\n    lang_rank_corr_globalmmlu_mmlupx: 0.69\n  result_rationale: 'Belebele partially replicates because it is high-quality and\n    natively multilingual. The 2.4pp transfer drop reflects domain shift (reading\n    comprehension vs knowledge). MMLU-ProX shows worse transfer (3.9pp drop) because\n    machine-translated MMLU introduces systematic distributional shifts (ambiguous\n    domain terms, cultural references, low-resource translation errors). CaCLR remains\n    worse than linear projection on both benchmarks, confirming the negative result\n    generalizes. Language ranking is stable for Belebele (ρ = 0.83) but degrades for\n    MMLU-ProX (ρ = 0.69) due to translation quality variation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 15: Cross-Benchmark Generalization (19-Language Mean)'\n    columns:\n    - benchmark\n    - zs_auroc_qwen3\n    - zs_auroc_llama4\n    - caclr_vs_linproj_pp\n    - lang_rank_corr\n    rows:\n    - - GlobalMMLU\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.588\n      - -1.3\n      - —\n    - - Belebele\n      - 0.594\n      - 0.567\n      - -0.8\n      - 0.83\n    - - MMLU-ProX\n      - 0.579\n      - 0.551\n      - -1.1\n      - 0.69\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S2 (supp): Three-panel scatter showing per-language AUROC correlation\n    across benchmark pairs. GlobalMMLU–Belebele tight; GlobalMMLU–MMLU-ProX noisier.\n    Low-resource languages (red) are outliers on MMLU-ProX.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Findings partially generalize: Belebele confirms, MMLU-ProX is noisier.\n    Language ranking stable but absolute performance degrades with domain shift. CaCLR''s\n    negative result is robust across all three benchmarks. Include Belebele in main\n    results, MMLU-ProX in supplementary with translation-artifact caveat.\n\n    '\n- name: INT8 Validation Ablation\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Compare INT8 vs FP16 probing results at 5 representative layers for\n    Llama 4 Scout on 5% random subset. Validates quantization doesn''t distort correctness\n    signals.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    int8_vs_fp16_auroc_delta_mean_pp: -0.3\n    int8_vs_fp16_auroc_delta_max_pp: -0.7\n    int8_vs_fp16_ece_delta_mean_pp: 0.2\n    int8_vs_fp16_activation_cosine_sim_mean: 0.994\n    int8_vs_fp16_rank_correlation_layers: 0.98\n    validation_passes: true\n  result_rationale: 'INT8 quantization introduces minimal distortion. The 0.3pp mean\n    AUROC difference is within noise — INT8 preserves directional structure of activations\n    that linear probes rely on. Cosine similarity 0.994 between INT8 and FP16 activations\n    confirms near-perfect directional alignment. Layer ranking is preserved (rank\n    corr 0.98). This passes cleanly even in the worst tier — quantization is not a\n    source of failure.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table S1: INT8 vs FP16 Comparison (Llama 4 Scout, 5% subset)'\n    columns:\n    - layer\n    - fp16_auroc\n    - int8_auroc\n    - delta_pp\n    - activation_cosine\n    rows:\n    - - 8\n      - 0.637\n      - 0.633\n      - -0.4\n      - 0.993\n    - - 16\n      - 0.7\n      - 0.697\n      - -0.3\n      - 0.994\n    - - 24\n      - 0.731\n      - 0.729\n      - -0.2\n      - 0.995\n    - - 32\n      - 0.746\n      - 0.743\n      - -0.3\n      - 0.994\n    - - 40\n      - 0.748\n      - 0.741\n      - -0.7\n      - 0.992\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S3 (supp): Paired scatter of FP16 vs INT8 AUROC at all layer × language\n    combinations. Points on y=x diagonal with slight downward bias. Inset: delta histogram\n    centered near −0.3pp.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'INT8 validation passes. Quantization is not a confound. One paragraph\n    + Table S1 in supplementary.\n\n    '\n- name: CKA Cross-Lingual Representation Similarity Analysis\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Compute CKA (linear kernel) between English and each target language\n    at every probed layer for both models. Correlate CKA with transfer AUROC to provide\n    mechanistic explanation for the Pareto frontier. Full-scale validation of pilot\n    finding (revised hypothesis: threshold model — transfer collapses only below critical\n    CKA threshold ~0.35). Report CKA × layer × language heatmaps, CKA vs transfer\n    degradation correlation per language, and threshold analysis.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    cka_en_vs_target_mean_all: 0.72\n    cka_decline_with_depth: true\n    cka_shallow_mean: 0.89\n    cka_deep_mean: 0.54\n    cka_vs_transfer_auroc_pearson_r_overall: 0.32\n    cka_vs_transfer_auroc_pearson_r_range_per_lang:\n    - -0.08\n    - 0.71\n    cka_vs_transfer_pearson_significant_langs: 7\n    cka_vs_transfer_pearson_nonsig_langs: 12\n    cka_threshold_0p35_replicates: partial\n    n_lang_layer_pairs_below_threshold: 18\n    transfer_collapse_below_threshold_pct: 0.61\n    transfer_collapse_above_threshold_pct: 0.22\n    cka_architecture_consistency: weak\n    cka_pearson_qwen3: 0.38\n    cka_pearson_llama4: 0.24\n    cka_by_tier:\n      high_resource_mean: 0.76\n      mid_resource_mean: 0.71\n      low_resource_mean: 0.64\n    cka_per_lang_deep_layer:\n      zh: 0.68\n      fr: 0.71\n      de: 0.69\n      es: 0.67\n      ja: 0.62\n      ko: 0.6\n      ru: 0.58\n      ar: 0.53\n      hi: 0.51\n      vi: 0.48\n      id: 0.52\n      th: 0.45\n      pl: 0.55\n      uk: 0.54\n      bn: 0.41\n      te: 0.39\n      ta: 0.37\n      sw: 0.33\n      yo: 0.28\n    pilot_revision_validated: partially — threshold exists but is noisy\n  result_rationale: 'CKA declines with depth on both models (shallow 0.89 → deep 0.54),\n    confirming that deeper layers are less cross-lingually aligned. However, the correlation\n    between CKA and transfer AUROC is weak overall (Pearson r = 0.32, explaining ~10%\n    of variance). This happens because the pilot''s revised hypothesis — that transfer\n    collapses only below a critical CKA threshold (~0.35) rather than declining linearly\n    — partially replicates but with too much noise at full scale. Below the 0.35 threshold,\n    61% of language-layer pairs show transfer collapse (AUROC < 0.55), but 39% do\n    not — meaning the threshold is suggestive, not deterministic. Above the threshold,\n    22% of pairs still show poor transfer, indicating CKA captures necessary but not\n    sufficient conditions for transfer. Per-language correlations vary wildly: hi\n    (r = 0.71, sig) and sw (r = 0.67, sig) show strong CKA-transfer coupling, but\n    zh (r = 0.12, ns) and fr (r = 0.08, ns) show near-zero correlation because these\n    high-resource languages transfer well regardless of CKA level. Architecture inconsistency\n    is notable: Qwen3 r = 0.38, Llama4 r = 0.24 — the mechanistic explanation is model-dependent.\n    For the paper, CKA provides a \"suggestive but not convincing\" mechanistic account:\n    geometric misalignment (lower CKA) correlates with transfer failure, but the relationship\n    is too noisy and architecture-dependent to serve as a reliable predictor or principled\n    layer selection criterion.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 16: CKA vs Transfer AUROC Correlation by Architecture'\n    columns:\n    - model\n    - pearson_r\n    - p_value\n    - n_sig_langs\n    - n_total_langs\n    - spearman_rho\n    rows:\n    - - Qwen3-235B\n      - 0.38\n      - 0.002\n      - 5\n      - 19\n      - 0.35\n    - - Llama4-Scout\n      - 0.24\n      - 0.041\n      - 2\n      - 19\n      - 0.21\n    - - Combined\n      - 0.32\n      - 0.001\n      - 7\n      - 38\n      - 0.29\n  - name: 'Table 17: CKA Threshold Analysis (threshold = 0.35)'\n    columns:\n    - condition\n    - n_pairs\n    - mean_transfer_auroc\n    - pct_below_0p55\n    - pct_above_0p65\n    rows:\n    - - CKA < 0.35\n      - 18\n      - 0.538\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.11\n    - - CKA ≥ 0.35\n      - 438\n      - 0.612\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.39\n  - name: 'Table 18: CKA at Deep Layers (75% depth) by Language'\n    columns:\n    - language\n    - resource_tier\n    - cka_qwen3\n    - cka_llama4\n    - transfer_auroc_qwen3\n    - transfer_auroc_llama4\n    rows:\n    - - zh\n      - high\n      - 0.71\n      - 0.65\n      - 0.668\n      - 0.658\n    - - fr\n      - high\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.672\n      - 0.662\n    - - de\n      - high\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.66\n      - 0.671\n      - 0.655\n    - - ja\n      - high\n      - 0.65\n      - 0.59\n      - 0.658\n      - 0.634\n    - - ru\n      - high\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.652\n      - 0.622\n    - - ar\n      - mid\n      - 0.56\n      - 0.5\n      - 0.625\n      - 0.589\n    - - hi\n      - mid\n      - 0.54\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.586\n    - - th\n      - mid\n      - 0.48\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.594\n      - 0.558\n    - - sw\n      - low\n      - 0.36\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.537\n      - 0.518\n    - - yo\n      - low\n      - 0.31\n      - 0.25\n      - 0.512\n      - 0.492\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 6a: CKA heatmap (y: layers, x: languages sorted by resource tier) for\n    Qwen3. Clear gradient: top-left (shallow × high-resource) is hot (CKA > 0.85);\n    bottom-right (deep × low-resource) is cool (CKA < 0.40). But the mid-region is\n    noisy — no clean contour separating transfer success from failure.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 6b: Scatter of CKA (x) vs transfer AUROC (y), one point per language-layer\n    pair, colored by model. Weak positive trend (r = 0.32). Vertical dashed line at\n    CKA = 0.35 (pilot threshold). Below threshold: most points cluster low-AUROC,\n    but some outliers transfer well. Above threshold: wide spread. The scatter is\n    too noisy for CKA to serve as a reliable predictor.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 6c: Per-language CKA-transfer correlation bars, sorted by magnitude. Hi\n    and sw show strong correlations (r > 0.6); zh and fr near zero. Architecture-colored\n    bars show Qwen3 correlations consistently higher than Llama4.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'CKA provides suggestive but not convincing mechanistic explanation. The\n    decline with depth is clear, and the threshold model partially replicates (61%\n    transfer collapse below CKA 0.35). But the correlation is too weak (r = 0.32)\n    and architecture-dependent (0.38 vs 0.24) to serve as a reliable predictor. The\n    key insight survives at a qualitative level: cross-lingual misalignment is geometric,\n    which explains why MMD-based CaCLR fails (it matches distributions but can''t\n    correct rotation) while linear projection succeeds (it learns the rotation). But\n    the quantitative story is messy. For the paper: report CKA as supporting evidence\n    for the geometric-misalignment interpretation, not as a standalone finding. Two\n    paragraphs in the analysis section.\n\n    '\n- name: Linear Projection Sensitivity Analysis\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: 'Vary number of parallel pairs (10, 20, 50, 100, 200) for linear projection\n    transfer. Determines minimum data requirement for this baseline. Both models ×\n    5 representative layers × 19 target languages.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    linproj_by_n_pairs:\n      10:\n        auroc_mean: 0.608\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: -1.0\n        pct_langs_improved: 0.26\n      20:\n        auroc_mean: 0.621\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.3\n        pct_langs_improved: 0.42\n      50:\n        auroc_mean: 0.636\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.8\n        pct_langs_improved: 0.55\n      100:\n        auroc_mean: 0.643\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 2.5\n        pct_langs_improved: 0.63\n      200:\n        auroc_mean: 0.647\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 2.9\n        pct_langs_improved: 0.68\n    minimum_viable_pairs_high_resource: 20\n    minimum_viable_pairs_mid_resource: 50\n    minimum_viable_pairs_low_resource: '>200 (insufficient quality)'\n    saturation_point: ~100 pairs — diminishing returns beyond\n    linproj_fails_low_resource: true\n    low_resource_at_200_pairs:\n      bn:\n        auroc: 0.561\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.8\n      te:\n        auroc: 0.576\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.5\n      ta:\n        auroc: 0.571\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.3\n      sw:\n        auroc: 0.543\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.6\n      yo:\n        auroc: 0.518\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 0.6\n    high_resource_at_50_pairs:\n      zh:\n        auroc: 0.681\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.3\n      fr:\n        auroc: 0.686\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.4\n      de:\n        auroc: 0.684\n        delta_vs_zs_pp: 1.3\n    variance_across_random_subsets_std_pp: 1.8\n  result_rationale: 'Linear projection''s effectiveness depends heavily on the number\n    and quality of parallel pairs. At 10 pairs, the affine transformation is underestimated\n    (high-dimensional activation space needs more samples to estimate rotation matrix\n    reliably), actually hurting transfer (−1.0pp) by introducing noise. At 20 pairs,\n    high-resource languages start benefiting because their parallel pairs have high\n    translation quality and the activation subspaces are already partially aligned.\n    At 50 pairs, the projection stabilizes for high and mid-resource languages (the\n    \"default\" setting in the main experiments). But low-resource languages fail even\n    at 200 pairs for two reasons: (1) GlobalMMLU parallel translations for sw/yo/bn\n    are lower quality (more literal, less natural), so the parallel activations don''t\n    capture the true language-specific encoding; (2) the activation subspace for these\n    languages is itself noisier (less structured due to limited pretraining), making\n    the rotation hard to estimate even with many pairs. The variance across random\n    subsets (std 1.8pp) is high, indicating the projection is sensitive to which specific\n    pairs are used — this is a practical reliability concern. Saturation at ~100 pairs\n    means the projection captures most learnable alignment, and remaining transfer\n    gap is due to fundamental representational differences, not data limitation.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 19: Linear Projection AUROC by Number of Parallel Pairs (19-Lang\n      Mean)'\n    columns:\n    - n_pairs\n    - auroc_mean\n    - auroc_std\n    - delta_vs_zs_pp\n    - pct_langs_improved\n    - pct_langs_hurt\n    rows:\n    - - 10\n      - 0.608\n      - 0.052\n      - -1.0\n      - 0.26\n      - 0.53\n    - - 20\n      - 0.621\n      - 0.047\n      - 0.3\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.32\n    - - 50\n      - 0.636\n      - 0.042\n      - 1.8\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.21\n    - - 100\n      - 0.643\n      - 0.04\n      - 2.5\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.16\n    - - 200\n      - 0.647\n      - 0.039\n      - 2.9\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.11\n  - name: 'Table 20: Linear Projection at 50 Pairs by Resource Tier'\n    columns:\n    - resource_tier\n    - n_langs\n    - auroc_mean\n    - delta_vs_zs_pp\n    - pct_improved\n    rows:\n    - - high\n      - 7\n      - 0.677\n      - 3.1\n      - 0.86\n    - - mid\n      - 7\n      - 0.631\n      - 1.6\n      - 0.57\n    - - low\n      - 5\n      - 0.553\n      - 0.1\n      - 0.2\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 7a: Learning curve — linproj AUROC (y) vs n_pairs (x, log scale), one\n    line per resource tier. High-resource rises steeply and saturates at ~50. Mid-resource\n    rises gently, saturates at ~100. Low-resource is flat — near-zero improvement\n    at any sample size. Shaded bands show ±1 std across random subsets.\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 7b: Paired delta (linproj − ZS) bar chart at n=50, sorted by language\n    resource tier. High-resource bars positive (1–4pp). Mid-resource mixed. Low-resource\n    near zero or negative. Illustrates that linear projection has a resource-tier\n    ceiling.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Linear projection is the strongest zero-label method but has a clear\n    resource-tier limitation. High-resource languages benefit reliably from ~20 pairs;\n    mid-resource need ~50; low-resource fail even at 200 because parallel quality\n    and activation structure are insufficient. For the paper, this strengthens the\n    practical recommendation: \"few-shot (25–100 labels) is the viable path for mid/low-resource;\n    linear projection works only for high-resource languages with quality parallel\n    data.\" The minimum-pairs analysis is a practical contribution that helps practitioners\n    decide between linproj and few-shot.\n\n    '\n- name: Probing Control Tasks (Hewitt & Liang 2019)\n  plan_id: plan_013\n  description: 'Train probes on randomly permuted labels at each layer. Compare selectivity\n    (probe AUROC − control AUROC) across layers and languages. Validates that high\n    AUROC reflects genuine correctness direction, not memorized dataset artifacts\n    or probe capacity.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    control_auroc_mean_en_qwen3: 0.548\n    control_auroc_mean_en_llama4: 0.544\n    control_auroc_range_en:\n    - 0.512\n    - 0.571\n    selectivity_en_best_layer_qwen3: 0.228\n    selectivity_en_best_layer_llama4: 0.205\n    selectivity_en_mean_all_layers: 0.168\n    selectivity_min_across_all: 0.048\n    selectivity_min_language: yo\n    selectivity_min_layer: shallow (L4/L8)\n    selectivity_by_resource_tier:\n      high_resource_mean: 0.172\n      mid_resource_mean: 0.138\n      low_resource_mean: 0.084\n    low_resource_selectivity_below_8pp:\n      sw_L4: 0.052\n      yo_L4: 0.048\n      yo_L8: 0.056\n      bn_L4: 0.061\n      ta_L4: 0.058\n      sw_L8: 0.063\n    pct_lang_layer_pairs_selectivity_below_8pp: 0.08\n    pct_low_resource_pairs_selectivity_below_8pp: 0.28\n    selectivity_depth_gradient: positive — deeper layers have higher selectivity\n    selectivity_vs_auroc_correlation: 0.74\n  result_rationale: 'Control tasks reveal that probe capacity explains a non-trivial\n    portion of AUROC, especially for low-resource languages at shallow layers. The\n    control AUROC (probes on random labels) averages 0.548 for English — above the\n    theoretical 0.50 baseline because logistic regression on high-dimensional activation\n    spaces (d > 4096) can memorize patterns in random labels when sample sizes are\n    small (GlobalMMLU has ~300–600 items per language). This means an AUROC of 0.60\n    at a shallow layer for a low-resource language has selectivity of only ~5pp —\n    dangerously close to the control. Specifically, 28% of low-resource language-layer\n    pairs have selectivity below 8pp, meaning a non-negligible fraction of \"successful\"\n    transfer AUROC in low-resource settings is probe capacity artifact rather than\n    genuine correctness signal. The selectivity minimum of 4.8pp (Yoruba at L4) means\n    some reported AUROCs are nearly indistinguishable from memorization. Deeper layers\n    show higher selectivity because the correctness signal strengthens with depth\n    while control AUROC stays constant (~0.55). The resource-tier gradient in selectivity\n    (high: 17.2pp, mid: 13.8pp, low: 8.4pp) mirrors the transfer gradient — low-resource\n    languages have both lower AUROC and lower selectivity, compounding the interpretive\n    challenge. For the paper, this means low-resource AUROC numbers should be presented\n    with selectivity-adjusted confidence intervals. Any language-layer pair with selectivity\n    < 8pp should be flagged as unreliable.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 21: Control Task Selectivity by Resource Tier'\n    columns:\n    - resource_tier\n    - n_langs\n    - probe_auroc_mean\n    - control_auroc_mean\n    - selectivity_mean_pp\n    - selectivity_min_pp\n    rows:\n    - - high\n      - 7\n      - 0.66\n      - 0.548\n      - 17.2\n      - 10.4\n    - - mid\n      - 7\n      - 0.614\n      - 0.546\n      - 13.8\n      - 8.2\n    - - low\n      - 5\n      - 0.548\n      - 0.544\n      - 8.4\n      - 4.8\n    - - all\n      - 19\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.546\n      - 13.8\n      - 4.8\n  - name: 'Table 22: Selectivity at Best English Layer (L72 Qwen3 / L36 Llama4)'\n    columns:\n    - language\n    - probe_auroc\n    - control_auroc\n    - selectivity_pp\n    - flag\n    rows:\n    - - en\n      - 0.776\n      - 0.548\n      - 22.8\n      - ''\n    - - zh\n      - 0.641\n      - 0.549\n      - 9.2\n      - ''\n    - - fr\n      - 0.645\n      - 0.548\n      - 9.7\n      - ''\n    - - de\n      - 0.644\n      - 0.549\n      - 9.5\n      - ''\n    - - ar\n      - 0.598\n      - 0.547\n      - 5.1\n      - ⚠ low\n    - - hi\n      - 0.591\n      - 0.546\n      - 4.5\n      - ⚠ low\n    - - sw\n      - 0.508\n      - 0.552\n      - -4.4\n      - ✗ below control\n    - - yo\n      - 0.487\n      - 0.551\n      - -6.4\n      - ✗ below control\n  - name: 'Table 23: Selectivity by Depth Band (19-Language Mean)'\n    columns:\n    - depth_band\n    - probe_auroc_mean\n    - control_auroc_mean\n    - selectivity_pp\n    rows:\n    - - 0–25%\n      - 0.582\n      - 0.549\n      - 3.3\n    - - 25–50%\n      - 0.618\n      - 0.547\n      - 7.1\n    - - 50–75%\n      - 0.641\n      - 0.546\n      - 9.5\n    - - 75–100%\n      - 0.634\n      - 0.548\n      - 8.6\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 8a: Selectivity heatmap (y: layers, x: languages by resource tier). Hot\n    (green, selectivity > 15pp) in upper-left (deep × high-resource). Cool (red, selectivity\n    < 8pp) in lower-right (shallow × low-resource). Several low-resource cells at\n    shallow layers are below zero (probe worse than control).\n\n    '\n  - 'Figure 8b: Scatter of selectivity (y) vs probe AUROC (x), one point per language-layer\n    pair. Strong positive correlation (r = 0.74). Horizontal dashed line at selectivity\n    = 8pp (reliability threshold). Vertical dashed line at AUROC = 0.65 (pass threshold).\n    Bottom-left quadrant (low selectivity + low AUROC) contains most low-resource\n    shallow pairs — these are unreliable and should not be interpreted as evidence\n    of transfer.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Control tasks are the most damaging experiment for the worst tier. They\n    reveal that 28% of low-resource language-layer pairs have selectivity < 8pp —\n    some \"transfer success\" in these settings is probe capacity artifact. At the English-optimal\n    layer, Swahili and Yoruba actually score BELOW the control, meaning the probe\n    is memorizing random structure rather than detecting correctness. This doesn''t\n    invalidate the main results (high-resource selectivity is robust at 17pp+), but\n    it qualifies them: the resource-tier taxonomy should be presented with selectivity\n    as a reliability measure. For the paper: \"Control tasks confirm that probe AUROC\n    for high/mid-resource languages reflects genuine correctness signals (selectivity\n    > 10pp), but low-resource results at shallow/moderate layers should be interpreted\n    cautiously (selectivity 3–8pp). The practical implication reinforces the few-shot\n    recommendation: for low-resource languages, even the probe AUROC metric itself\n    is unreliable without labeled data to establish a non-trivial baseline.\"\n\n    '\n- name: Gemma 4 31B Dense — Third Architecture (Camera-Ready)\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Full Pareto frontier on Gemma 4 31B Dense (60 layers, dense, FP16).\n    20+ languages. Dense vs MoE architectural contrast.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    en_auroc_best_layer: 0.761\n    en_best_layer_idx: 45\n    en_auroc_range:\n    - 0.601\n    - 0.761\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_19lang: 0.605\n    zs_transfer_drop_mean_pp: 17.1\n    zs_langs_above_0p65: 6\n    pareto_global_spearman_rho: -0.44\n    pareto_per_lang_rho_median: -0.31\n    pareto_rho_sign_consistency: 0.68\n    pareto_frontier_n_nondominated: 6\n    pareto_frontier_agreement_with_qwen3_tau: 0.42\n    pareto_frontier_agreement_with_llama4_tau: 0.29\n    caclr_auroc_mean: 0.609\n    caclr_vs_linproj_pp: -0.9\n    dense_vs_moe_finding: 'Dense architecture shows cleaner Pareto frontier (ρ = −0.44\n      vs −0.38/−0.18 for MoE) with higher sign consistency (0.68 vs 0.63/0.53). MoE\n      routing is a source of non-monotonic noise, but the frontier is still moderate\n      even for dense models.\n\n      '\n  result_rationale: 'Gemma 4 31B Dense shows a cleaner but still moderate Pareto frontier\n    because dense architectures have more homogeneous layer transitions (no expert\n    routing to create non-monotonic jumps). The Spearman ρ of −0.44 is the highest\n    among three architectures but still means depth explains only ~19% of transfer\n    variance. English AUROC 0.761 is between Qwen3 (0.776) and Llama4 (0.749), consistent\n    with 31B active parameters being intermediate. Transfer is intermediate too (0.605).\n    Cross-architecture agreement remains low (τ = 0.42 with Qwen3, 0.29 with Llama4),\n    confirming architecture-dependence even between dense and MoE models. CaCLR still\n    fails on this architecture, ruling out the hypothesis that MoE routing specifically\n    caused CaCLR''s failure.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 24: Three-Architecture Comparison Summary'\n    columns:\n    - model\n    - type\n    - layers\n    - en_auroc\n    - zs_auroc_mean\n    - pareto_rho\n    - sign_consistency\n    - pareto_agreement_mean_tau\n    rows:\n    - - Qwen3-235B\n      - MoE\n      - 94\n      - 0.776\n      - 0.618\n      - -0.38\n      - 0.63\n      - 0.38\n    - - Llama4-Scout\n      - MoE\n      - 48\n      - 0.749\n      - 0.588\n      - -0.18\n      - 0.53\n      - 0.31\n    - - Gemma4-31B\n      - Dense\n      - 60\n      - 0.761\n      - 0.605\n      - -0.44\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.36\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 9 (camera-ready): Three-panel Pareto frontier comparison (Qwen3, Llama4,\n    Gemma4). Dense model''s frontier is more visually coherent but still noisy. MoE\n    models show increasing scatter. Annotated with ρ values and sign consistency.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Gemma4 confirms the diagnostic paper''s thesis from a new angle: MoE\n    routing adds non-monotonic noise to the Pareto frontier, but even the dense architecture''s\n    frontier is moderate (ρ = −0.44). The depth-transfer tradeoff is real but explains\n    limited variance regardless of architecture. Cross-architecture agreement remains\n    low, confirming architecture-dependence.\n\n    '\n- name: Extended Language Coverage (50+ Languages, Camera-Ready)\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Expand from 20 to 50+ languages using full GlobalMMLU/Belebele language\n    sets.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_languages: 52\n    zs_transfer_auroc_mean_51lang: 0.581\n    zs_transfer_auroc_median_51lang: 0.574\n    zs_pass_rate_51lang: 0.24\n    low_resource_auroc_mean_new_langs: 0.541\n    worst_language_auroc: 0.488\n    worst_language: Amharic (am)\n    resource_tier_gradient_steeper: true\n    new_language_families_added: 5\n    transfer_by_tier_expanded:\n      high:\n        n: 10\n        auroc_mean: 0.651\n        pass_rate: 0.7\n      mid:\n        n: 18\n        auroc_mean: 0.598\n        pass_rate: 0.28\n      low:\n        n: 14\n        auroc_mean: 0.553\n        pass_rate: 0.07\n      very_low:\n        n: 10\n        auroc_mean: 0.518\n        pass_rate: 0.0\n  result_rationale: 'Expanding to 52 languages worsens the aggregate picture because\n    additional languages are predominantly low and very-low-resource. Many (Amharic,\n    Lao, Mongolian, Georgian) have minimal representation in pretraining data, so\n    their activations share even less structure with English. Pass rate drops from\n    ~37% (7/19) to ~24% (12/51). Some languages approach chance (AUROC ~0.49). The\n    resource-tier gradient steepens: very-low-resource languages (n=10) average 0.518\n    with 0% pass rate. This strengthens the diagnostic message: probe-based calibration\n    has a severe language barrier.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 25: Transfer Success by Resource Tier (52-Language Set, Qwen3)'\n    columns:\n    - resource_tier\n    - n_langs\n    - zs_auroc_mean\n    - zs_auroc_std\n    - pass_rate\n    rows:\n    - - high\n      - 10\n      - 0.651\n      - 0.017\n      - 0.7\n    - - mid\n      - 18\n      - 0.598\n      - 0.023\n      - 0.28\n    - - low\n      - 14\n      - 0.553\n      - 0.028\n      - 0.07\n    - - very_low\n      - 10\n      - 0.518\n      - 0.021\n      - 0.0\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 10 (camera-ready): World map heatmap with 52 languages colored by ZS transfer\n    AUROC. Europe and East Asia green/yellow. South/SE Asia orange. Sub-Saharan Africa\n    and Central Asia red (near-chance). Reinforces the \"language barrier\" framing.\n\n    '\n  analysis: 'Extended coverage is the strongest evidence for the diagnostic conclusion.\n    With 52 languages, the majority fail to transfer. Very-low-resource languages\n    (10 langs, AUROC 0.518) are indistinguishable from chance when accounting for\n    control task selectivity. The paper''s conclusion: \"probe-based calibration does\n    not generalize beyond a small set of high-resource languages without fundamental\n    methodological advances.\"'\n","numLines":2096,"startLine":400,"totalLines":2495}}}
